<OSI Model Layers>
*** NOTES ***
$ OSI Model
- Physical, Data Link, Network, Transport, Session, Presentation, Application
- Data is encapsulated top-down (Application -> Physical) when sending
- Data is decapsulated bottom-up (Physical -> Application) when receiving

$ Layer 7 - Application
- Purpose: Provides network services directly to the end user's applications. (Not the application, but the services it uses (e.g., HTTP)
- Functions:
	- Defines how apps access network resources
	- Manages high-level protocols, user interfaces, and data exchange.
- Protocols/Examples:
	- HTTP/HTTPS -> Web Browsing
	- SMTP, POP3, IMAP -> Email
	- FTP, SFTP, TFTP -> File Transfers
	- DNS -> Resolves names to IPs
- Devices: proxies, application gateways, firewalls with deep packet inspection
- Headers: Application data (user data, like HTML, email body)

$ Layer 6 - Presentation
- Purpose: Formatting, compression, and encryption of data so the receiving application understands it
- Functions:
	- Translation: Different systems use different encoding; presentation ensures compatibility.
	- Encryption/Decryption: SSL/TLS handshake
	- Compression: JPEG, MP3, MPEG
- Examples:
	- SSL/TLS (though often described as layer 5-7 depending on context)
	- ASCII, Unicode
	- JPEG, GIF, MP3
- Devices: SSL accelerators, gateways
? Which layer in the OSI model provides encryption ->  Presentation Layer

$ Layer 5 - Session
- Purpose: Manages dialogue (sessions) between applications. Sets up, manages, and terminates sessions.
- Functions:
	- Establish, maintain, and end connections (logon/logoff)
	- Synchronization checkpoints (e.g., resume a file transfer)
- Examples: 
	- NetBIOS
	- RPC
	- PPTP
	- SMB session control
- Devices:
	- Session border controllers (VoIP)

$ Layer 4 - Transport
- Purpose: Provides reliable (or unreliable) delivery of data between hosts.
- Functions: 
	- Segmentation: Splits data into smaller units for transmission
	- Reliability: Ensures all segments arrive (if using TCP)
	- Flow control: Prevents overwhelming the receiver
	- Error detection/recovery: Retransmit lost packets.
- Key Protocols
	- TCP: Connection-oriented, reliable, sequencing, acknowledgements, error recovery
	- UDP: Connectionless, no guarantees, faster
- Headers:
	- TCP/UDP headers with port numbers
- Devices:
	- Firewalls
	- Load balancers

$ Layer 3 - Network
- Purpose: Responsible for logical addressing and routing packets across networks
- Functions:
	- Logical Addressing (IP addresses)
	- Routing and forwarding
	- Fragmentation (splitting packets to fit MTU)
- Protocols/Examples:
	- IPv4/IPv6
	- ICMP (ping, traceroute)
	- ARP (though often considered between 2 & 3)
	- OSPF, RIP, EIGRP, BGP (routing protocols)
- Devices: 
	- Routers
	- Layer 3 switches
	- Firewalls (at IP layer)
- Headers: Network header contains source and destination IP addresses

$ Layer 2 - Data Link
- Purpose: Provides physical addressing and reliable communication over a physical medium
- Sub-layers:
	- LLC (Logical Link Control): Error checking, flow control
	- MAC: deciding who can talk when on a shared medium
- Protocols/Examples:
	- Ethernet (802.3)
	- Wi-Fi (802.11)
	- PPP, HDLC
- Devices:
	- Switches
	- Bridges
	- NICs
	- WAPs
- Headers: Frame header/trailer with MAC source/destination + error check (FCS)

$ Layer 1 - Physical
- Purpose: Transmits raw bits (0s and 1s) over a medium. Defines physical aspects of transmission
- Functions:
	- Bit representation (voltage, light pulses, radio waves)
	- Cable specifications (UTP, fiber, coax)
	- Connectors (RJ45, RJ11)
	- Data rate (bps)
- Examples:
	- Ethernet physical layer specs
	- Wi-Fi radio frequencies
	- DSL, ISDN
- Devices: 
	- Hubs
	- Repeaters
	- Cables
	- Connectors

$ Encapsulation - How OSI Layers Work Together
- L7: user data created (HTTP request)
- L6: encryption, compression, formatting
- L5: session management info added
- L4: segment header added (ports, TCP/UDP)
- L3: packet header added (IP source/destination)
- L2: frame header/trailer added (MAC addresses, FCS)
- L1: bits put onto the wire

<Networking Appliances>
*** NOTES ***
$ Router
- Definition: A layer 3 devices that forwards packets between different networks using IP addressing
- How it works:
	- Looks at destination IP address
	- Uses routing tables + protocols (OSPF, BGP, RIP)
- Pros:
	- Connects LANs/WANs
	- Can do NAT, DHCP, ACLs
- Cons:
	- Slower than switches (more processing)
- Examples:
	- Home router (Wi-Fi + NAT)
	- Enterprise edge router (Cisco ISR, Juniper)
* "Forwards packets between different networks" -> Router

$ Switch
- Definition: A layer 2 device that forwards frames based on MAC addresses
- How it works:
	- Builds MAC address table dynamically
	- Modern switches also do Layer 3 (routing)
- Pros:
	- Faster than hubs (unicast forwarding)
	- Supports VLANs
- Cons:
	- More expensive than hubs
- Examples:
	- LAN access switches (Cisco Catalyst)
	- Data center switches (Arista, Juniper)
* "forwards frames within a LAN" -> Switch
* Don't confuse with a hub (hub = broadcast everything)

$ Firewall
- Definition: Security device that filters traffic based on rules (ports, IPs, protocols, state)
- Types:
	- Packet-filtering firewall -> basic ACLs
	- Stateful firewall -> tracks sessions
	- Next-gen firewall (NGFW) -> adds DPI, IDS/IPS, app filtering
- Pros:
	- First line for defense
- Cons:
	- Single point of failure if not redundant
- Examples:
	- Palo Alto, Cisco ASA, Fortinet
* "Filters traffic based on port/protocol/IP" -> Firewall

$ IDS/IPS
- Definition: Intrusion Detection System / Intrusion Prevention System
	- IDS = Detect only (alert)
	- IPS = Detect + Prevent (block)
- How it works:
	- Signature-based (known patterns)
	- Behavior-based (anomalies)
- Pros:
	- IDS = non-intrusive monitoring
	- IPS = real-time blocking
- Cons:
	- IDS = can't stop attack
	- IPS = may block false positives
- Examples:
	- Snort, Suricata, Cisco Firepower

$ Load Balancer
- Definition: Distributes traffic across multiple servers to improve performance/reliability
- Types:
	- Layer 4 -> based on IP/port
	- Layer 7 -> based on application (HTTP headers, cookies)
- Pros:
	- High availability
	- Prevents overload
- Cons:
	Single point of failure if not redundant
- Examples:
	- AWS ELB, F5 BIG-IP, NGINX
* "Distributes client requests among multiple servers" -> Load balancer

$ Proxy Server
- Definition: Intermediary between client and server
- Types:
	- Forward Proxy -> client -> proxy -> internet
	- Reverse Proxy -> internet -> proxy -> internal server
- Pros:
	- Caching, anonymity, filtering
	- Security layer
- Cons:
	-Adds latency
- Examples:
	- Squid, Blue Coat, NGINX
* "Forward Proxy" = client anonymity
* "Reverse Proxy" = hides backend servers

$ NAS (Network Attached Storage)
- Definition: File-level storage device on a network
- Protocols: SMB/CIFS, NFS
- Pros:
	- Easy to share files
	- Centralized storage
- Examples:
	- Synology, QNAP appliances
* "NAS" == File-level storage

$ SAN (Storage Area Network)
- Definition: High-speed network dedicated to block-level storage
- Technologies: Fibre Channel, iSCSI, FCoE
- Pros:
	- Very fast, scalable, supports databases/VMs
- Cons:
	- Expensive, complex
- Examples:
	- Enterprise data centers (VMware clusters)
* "SAN" == Block-level, not file-level	



<Cloud Concepts>
$ NFV - Network Functions Virtualization
- Definition: The virtualization of traditional network services (like firewalls, load balances, routers) into software that runs on commodity hardware.
- How it works:
	- Instead of buying physical hardware, you spin up virtual appliances in the cloud or data center.
	- Managed through orchestrators like OpenStack or VMware NSX
- Real-world examples:
	- A virtual firewall instead of a Cisco ASA box.
	- A virtual load balancer like AWS Elastic Load Balancer (ELB)
* Don't confuse NFV with SDN (Software-Defined Networking)
	- NFV = virtual functions (services)
	- SDN = central control over networking

$ Virtual Private Cloud (VPC)
- Definition: A logically isolated private subnet in a public cloud provider's environment.
- How it works: 
	- You get a slice of AWS, Azure, or GCP with your own private IP ranges, routing tables, and gateways
	- Think of it as your own private data center inside a public cloud
- Examples:
	- AWS VPC
	- Azure Virtual Network (VNet)
	- Google VPC
* VPC = isolated private cloud environment | VPN = encrypted tunnel over public internet

$ NSG - Network Security Group
- Definition: A virtual firewall/filter for controlling inbound and outbound traffic to resources in a VPC
- How it works:
	- Rule-based: allow/deny based on source, destination, port, and protocol
	- Applied to subnets, NICs, or instances
- Examples:
	- Azure NSG
	- AWS Security Groups
* NSG != NACL
	- NSG = stateful (remembers sessions)
	- NACL = stateless (must allow both inbound and outbound separately)

$ Cloud Gateways
- Definition: Virtual gateways that connect your on-premises network to the cloud
- Types:
	- VPN Gateway -> encrypted tunnel to the cloud
	- Direct Connect/ExpressRoute -> dedicated private link
	- Application Gateways -> load balancing + SSL termination
- Examples:
	- AWS VPN Gateway
	- Azure application gateway
	- Google Cloud Cloud VPN / Interconnect
* "connect on-premises to cloud" == Cloud Gateway

$ Deployment Models
- Public Cloud
	- Provider owns everything, shared infrastructure (AWS, Azure, GCP)
	- Scalable, cheap, flexible
	- Less control
- Private Cloud
	- Infrastructure dedicated to a single organization (on-premises or hosted)
	- High security, compliance, control
	- Expensive
- Hybrid Cloud
	- Mix of public + private
	- Example: Company keeps sensitive data in private cloud, uses public cloud for scalability
- Community Cloud
	- Shared by multiple organizations with common goals (e.g, hospitals sharing medical cloud infrastructure)
* Scalability and cost savings -> Public
* Compliance and security -> Private
* Best of both worlds -> Hybrid
* Shared among similar orgs -> Community



$ Service Models - The "as-a-service" stack
- IaaS - Infrastructure as a Service
	- Virtualized infrastructure (servers, storage, networking)
	- You manage OS, apps; provider manages hardware
	- Example: AWS, EC2, Azure VM
	* "you manage the operating system"
- PaaS - Platform as a Service
	- Provider gives devs a ready platform (runtime, DB, middle-wear)
	- You only deploy the code
	- Example: AWS Elastic Beanstalk, Google App Engine
	- "Developers only focus on the app not the servers"
- SaaS - Software as a Service
	- Fully managed applications delivered via cloud.
	- Example: Gmail, Office 365, Salesforce
	- "End-user just uses the software"
	


<Ports and Protocols>
*** NOTES ***
$ FTP - File Transfer Protocol
- Ports: TCP 20 (data) & TCP 21 (control)
- Purpose: Transfers files between client and server
- Example: Uploading website files to a hosting server
- Pros: Simple, widely supported
- Cons: Insecure (credentials sent in plaintext)
- Note: Use SFTP or FTPS instead

$ SSH - Secure Shell
- Port: TCP 22
- Purpose: Secure remote login and encrypted communication
- Example: Admins managing linux servers remotely
- Pros: Encrypted (replaces Telnet)
- Cons: Needs client software (e.g, PuTTY)

$ SFTP - SSH File Transfer Protocol
- Port: TCP 22 (same as SSH)
- Purpose: Secure file transfers using SSH
- Example: Secure upload of backups or logs
- Pros: Encrypted end-to-end
- Cons: Requires SSH access
* Don't confuse SFTP with FTPS

$ Telnet
- Port: TCP 23
- Purpose: Remote terminal access (plaintext)
- Example: Legacy network device management
- Pros: Simple, universal
- Cons: Completely insecure (everything plaintext)

$ SMTP - Simple Mail Transfer Protocol
- Ports: TCP 25, TCP 587 (submission), TCP 465 (legacy secure)
- Purpose: Sends outbound email between clients and servers
- Example: Outlook sending email to Gmail's SMTP server
- Pros: Standard email sending
- Cons: Doesn't handle receiving email (that's POP3/IMAP)
* "Sending Email" == SMTP

$ DNS - Domain Name System
- Ports: UDP 53 (queries), TCP 53 (zone transfers)
- Purpose: Resolves domain names -> IP addresses
- Example: Translating www.google.com -> 142.250.190.14
- Pros: Human-friendly names
- Cons: DNS spoofing/poisoning attacks possible

$ DHCP - Dynamic Host Configuration Protocol
- Ports: UDP 67 (server), UDP 68 (client)
- Purpose: Automatically assigns IP addresses, subnet masks, and gateways
- Example: Your laptop joining Wi-Fi at a coffee shop
- Pros: No manual config needed
- Cons: Susceptible to rogue DHCP servers

$ HTTP - HyperText Transfer Protocol
- Port: TCP 80
- Purpose: Web browsing (unencrypted)
- Example: Loading a website without HTTPS
- Pros: Universal standard
- Cons: Insecure (plaintext)

$ HTTPS - HyperText Transfer Protocol Secure
- Port: TCP 443
- Purpose: Secure web browsing (TLS encryption)
- Example: Banking, online shopping, basically all modern web
- Pros: Confidentiality, Integrity, Authentication
- Cons: Requires certificate management

$ SNMP - Simple Network Management Protocol
- Ports: UDP 161 (queries), UDP 162 (traps)
- Purpose: Monitors and manages network devices
- Example: Network monitoring tools (Nagios, SolarWinds)
- Pros: Centralized monitoring
- Cons: SNMP v1/v2 = insecure (plaintext); SNMP v3 = encrypted
* "Network monitoring/management" == SNMP

$ LDAP - Lightweight Directory Access Protocol
- Ports: TCP/UDP 389, TCP 636 (LDAPS secure)
- Purpose: Directory services (stores user info, authentication)
- Example: Active Directory (Windows logins)
- Pros: Centralized user management
- Cons: LDAP (389) = insecure; LDAPS (636) = secure
* "active directory" or "directory services" = LDAP

$ RDP - Remote Desktop Protocol
- Port: TCP/UDP 3389
- Purpose: Remote desktop access with GUI
- Example: IT support accessing a user's Windows PC
- Pros: GUI
- Cons: Security risk if exposed to the internet

$ SIP - Session Initiation Protocol
- Ports: TCP/UDP 5060 (unencrypted), TCP/UDP 5061 (encrypted with TLS)
- Purpose: Sets up, manages, and tears down VoIP calls
- Example: Skype, Zoom, VoIP phones
- Pros: Flexible, scalable
- Cons: Only sets up sessions (actual media uses RTP)
* "VoIP call setup" == VoIP


<Traffic Types>
*** NOTES ***
$ Unicast
- Definition: One-to-one communication. A single sender transmits data to a specific receiver.
- How it works:
	- Destination = Single IP address
	- Common in most network traffic (web browsing, SSH, VoIP call between 2 people)
- Example: 
	- You type www.google.com into your browser
	- Your computer sends a unicast request to Google's server IP
	- Google responds directly to you.
- Pros:
	- Efficient for direct communication
	- Private (only sender and receiver see the traffic)
- Cons:
	- If you want to reach multiple users, you must send multiple unicasts -> Wastes bandwidth
* "Normal network traffic" == Unicast

$ Broadcast - IPv4 Only
- Definition: One-to-all communication. One sender transmits data to all devices in a broadcast domain
- How it works:
	- Destination = Broadcast address
	- Every device in that subnet receives it
- Example:
	- ARP requests ("Who has this IP?")
	- DHCP discover when a device first joins a network
- Pros:
	- Useful for service discovery
- Cons:
	- Consumes bandwidth -> every device has to process the broadcast, even if it doesn't care
	- Doesn't scale well on large networks
* IPv6 has no broadcast - instead, IPv6 uses multicast and anycast

$ Multicast
- Definition: One-to-many communication, but only to devices that subscribe to a multicast group
- How it works:
	- Destination = Multicast group IP (IPv4 Class D: 224.0.0.0 - 239.255.255.255)
	- Routers deliver packets only to devices that have joined the group.
- Example:
	- IPTV streaming
	- Online gaming updates to multiple players
	- Video conferencing
- Pros:
	- Efficient: sender only sends one stream -> network replicates it for subscribed receivers
- Cons:
	- Requires support form routers/switches
	- More complex than Unicast
* Class D addresses or "group communications" = Multicast

$ Anycast (IPv6 and some IPv4 with BGP)
- Definition: One-to-nearest communication. One sender, multiple possible receivers, but data is routed to the nearest/most optimal
- How it works:
	- Multiple devices share the same IP address
	- Routing decides which one is the "closest" (based on routing metrics, not geographic distance)
- Example:
	- DNS root servers (multiple servers worldwide use the same IP)
	- CDNs like Cloudflare, Akamai, Google
- Pros:
	- Load balancing
	- Faster response times
- Cons:
	- Only the closest server responds - not multiple
* "same IP at multiple locations", "routed to nearest server" = Anycast


<Transmission Media>
$ 802.11 Wi-Fi Standards
- 802.11a (1999) -> 5 GHz, 54 Mbps
- 802.11b (1999) -> 2.4 GHz, 11 Mbps
- 802.11g (2003) -> 2.4 GHz, 54Mbps
- 802.11n (2009) -> 2.4 GHz & 5 GHz, 600Mbps (MIMO) (Wi-Fi 4)
- 802.11ac (2013) -> 5 GHz, several Gbps (MU-MIMO) (Wi-Fi 5)
- 802.11ax (2019) -> 2.4 GHz, 5 GHz, 6 GHz, higher efficiency (OFDMA, MU-MIMO) (Wi-Fi 6)
- Pros:
	- Mobility, easy deployment
- Cons:
	- Interference, limited range, less secure than wired
* Wi-Fi 6E adds 6 GHz band

$ Cellular Networks
- Definition: Wireless communication via mobile carriers
- Generations:
	- 2G (GSM, CDMA) -> Voice, SMS
	- 3G -> Basic mobile Internet
	- 4G LTE -> Broadband speeds, HD video
	- 5G -> Ultra-low latency, IoT, gigabit + speeds
- Pros:
	- Wide coverage
	- High mobility
- Cons:
	- Dependent on carrier towers
	- Bandwidth varies with congestion
- Example:
	- Remote access, backup WAN links
* "IoT, low latency, gigabit speeds" -> 5G

$ Satellite
- Definition: Communication via orbiting satellites
- Pros: 
	- Global coverage (even remote areas)
- Cons:
	- High latency (geostationary ~600ms)
	- Weather interference (rain fade)
	- Expensive
- Example:
	- Rural internet, ships, aircraft, military
* "high latency" == Satellite

$ Wired Transmission Media

$ Fiber Optic
- Definition: Uses light over glass fibers
- Types:
	- Single-Mode (SMF)
		- Long distance (tens of km)
		- Expensive lasers, thin core
	- Multimode (MMF):
		- Short distance (hundreds of meters)
		- Cheaper LEDs, thicker core
- Pros:
	- Very high bandwidth
	- Immune to EMI
	- Long distance
- Cons:
	- Fragile, more expensive
- Example:
	- ISP backbones, data centers, long-haul links

$ Coaxial Cable
- Definition: Copper cable with central conductor, shield, and insulation
- Pros:
	- Better shielding than twisted pair
	- Supports legacy broadband and TV
- Cons:
	- Bulky, less flexible
	- Mostly legacy in enterprise
- Examples:
	- Cable Internet (DOCSIS)
	- CCTV Cameras
	- Old Ethernet (10BASE2, 10BASE5)
* DOCSIS -> coax

$ Direct Attach Copper (DAC)
- Definition: A twinax copper cable with transceivers permanently attached
- Pros:
	- High speed (10/25/40/100Gbps)
	- Low latency
	- Cheaper than fiber for short runs
- Cons:
	- Very short distance (1-7m)
- Example:
	- Data centers for switch-to-switch or switch-to-server connections
* "short distance, high speed interconnect inside rack" -> DAC



<Transceivers and Connectors>
*** NOTES ***
$ Fiber Connectors (SC, LC, ST, MPO)
- SC - Subscriber Connector / Standard Connector / Square Connector
	- Type: Fiber optic
	- Shape: Square, snap-in, push-pull connector
	- Size: Large compared to LC
	- Usage: Common in enterprise fiber networks with data centers
	- Characteristics:
		- Easy push-pull mechanism
		- Often used in pairs (Tx/Rx)
		- Good for patch panels
* "Square connector" or "snap-in fiber" == SC
- LC - Lucent Connector / Little Connector
	- Type: Fiber optic
	- Shape: Small, square, uses a latch/tab (like an RJ45 clip)
	- Size: Half the size of SC -> high-density usage
	- Usage: Modern networks, switches, SFP modules
	- Characteristics:
		- Duplex (pairs Tx/Rx in one clip)
		- Smaller form factor = fits more ports in a patch panel
* "high density fiber" or "small form connector" == LC
- ST - Straight Tip
	- Type: Fiber Optic
	- Shape: Round with bayonet twist-and-lock (like BNC)
	- Usage: Older fiber networks, legacy installations
	- Characteristics:
		- Push in, then twist to lock
		- Not as common in modern builds (LC/SC dominate now)
* "Twist-lock fiber connector" == ST
- MPO - Multi-Fiber Push On
	- Type: Fiber optic
	- Shape: Rectangular with a push-on latch
	- Usage: high-density, multi-fiber cables (40G, 100G, 400G Ethernet)
	- Characteristics:
		- Can carry 12, 24, or more fibers in one connector
		- used in data centers for backbone cabling
* "high-speed backbone" or "multi-fiber in one connector" == MPO

$ Copper Connectors (RJ11, RJ45, F-Type, BNC)
- RJ11
	- Type: Copper (twisted pair)
	- Pins: 4 or 6 positions (usually 2 or 4 wires used)
	- Usage: Telephony (landline phones, DSL modems)
	- Characteristics:
		- Narrower than RJ45
		- Not used for Ethernet
* "telephones" or "DSL" == RJ11
- RJ45
	- Type: Copper (twisted pair)
	- Pins: 8 positions (8 wires, 4 pairs)
	- Usage: Ethernet (Cat5e, Cat6, Cat6a, Cat7, Cat8)
	- Characteristics:
		- Standard for 10/100/1000 Mbps and above.
		- Uses T568A/T568B wiring standards
* "Ethernet", "UTP", "Cat cables" == RJ45
- F-Type
	- Type: Copper coaxial
	- Shape: Screw-on threaded connector
	- Usage: Cable TV, cable modems, satellite TV
	- Characteristics:
		- Provides tight connection for RF signals
		- Standard for residential coax service
* "cable modem" or "TV coax" == F-Type
- BNC - Bayonet Neill-Concelman
	- Type: Copper coaxial
	- Shape: Round with bayonet twist-lock
	- Usage: Old Ethernet (10Base2), CCTV cameras, some RF applications.
	- Characteristics:
		- Push in, twist to lock
		- Still seen in surveillance systems
* "coax + twist lock" == BNC



<Network Topologies>
$ Mesh
- Definition: Every device is connected to every other device
- Types: 
	- Full Mesh -> Every node has a link to every other node
	- Partial Mesh -> Some nodes fully connected, others partially
- Pros:
	- High redundancy -> if one link fails, traffic reroutes
	- Excellent fault tolerance
- Cons:
	- Expensive (cabling + hardware)
	- Exomples to manage at scale
- Examples:
	- WANs (MPLS, carrier networks)
	- Wireless mesh networks (IoT, smart cities)

$ Hybrid
- Definition: Combination of two or more topologies
- Pros: 
	- Flexibility - takes best of multiple designs
	- Scales easily
- Cons:
	- Complexity in design/management
- Examples:
	- Enterprise networks mixing star in LAN + mesh in WAN
	- Office LAN = star, backbone WAN = mesh -> that's hybrid

$ Star/Hub-and-Spoke
- Definitions: All devices connect to a central device (hub, switch, or router)
- Star (LAN) -> PCs connected to a central switch
- Hub-and_Spoke (WAN) -> Branch offices (spokes) connect to a central HQ (hub)
- Pros:
	- Simple, cheap, easy to expand
	- One link down = only that device is affected
- Cons:
	- Central device is a single point of failure
- Example: 
	- LANs (Ethernet star)
	- WANs (MPLS hub-and-spoke)
* Star = typical LAN
* Hub-and-Spoke = typical WAN

$ Spine and Leaf
- Definition: Data center design with two layers
	- Leaf switches -> connect to servers
	- Spine switches -> connect to all leafs
- Pros:
	- Predictability latency
	- Scales horizontally (add more spines/leaves)
	- Non-blocking = equal cost paths
- Cons:
	- Requires more cabling than simple star
- Examples:
	- Modern data centers (VMware NSX, Cisco ACI)
	- Cloud providers (AWS, Azure backbones)
* "data centers, equal paths, leaf/spine" == Spine and Leaf

$ Point-to-Point
- Definition: A single direct connection between two devices
- Pros:
	- simple, secure, fast
	- no intermediaries
- Cons: 
	- Doesn't scale - limited to 2 devices
- Examples:
	- WAN leased lines (T1, MPLS)
	- Direct wireless links (microwave, satellite)
* "two endpoints" == Point-to-Point

$ Three-Tier
- Definition: Traditional enterprise campus LAN design with 3 layers:
	- Core -> high-speed backbone (fast routing/switching)
	- Distribution -> Policy, routing between VLANs, security
	- Access -> end devices (PCs, printers, APIs)
- Pros:
	- Structured, scalable
	- Easier troubleshooting (clear separation of roles)
- Cons:
	- More expensive, more hardware
- Example:
	- Classic enterprise networks
* "Core/Distribution/Access" == Three-Tier

$ Collapsed Core
- Definition: Two-tier design that combines Core + Distribution into one layer, plus Access layer
- Why: Used in smaller networks to reduce cost/complexity
- Pros:
	- Cheaper, simpler
	- Still provides redundancy
- Cons:
	- Less scalable than full three-tier
- Example:
	- Small to medium-sized enterprise LANs
* "smaller environment" but "distribution + access" == Collapsed Core



<Ipv4 Addressing>
*** NOTES ***
$ Public vs. Private IPv4 addresses
- Ipv4 addresses are 32 bites long  
- Public IP: Internet-routable addresses that routers on the internet can forwards packets to
- Private IP: Not routable on the public internet and used in private networks
	- To reach the internet private addresses are translated via NAT on the edge device (router/firewall)
? Why use private addresses? - They conserve the public IP space, enable internal addressing schemes, and increase security.
- RFC1918 private ranges:
	- 10.0.0.0/8 == 10.0.0.0 to 10.255.255.255
	- 172.16.0.0/12 == 172.16.0.0 to 172.31.255.255
	- 192.168.0.0/16 == 192.168.0.0 to 192.168.255.255
* If asked "Which address is private?" pick from the above ranges
* /x just means the first x bits are fixed!

$ APIPA (Automatic Private IP Addressing
- Range 169.254.0.0/16 (169.254.0.0 to 169.254.255.255)
- Used by Windows and other OSes when DHCP fails and no static IP exists. 
	- It gives the host an address so it can talk to other local hosts on the same L2 link that also have APIPA addresses.
	- NOT routable across routers
* APIPA = DHCP fail fallback. If you see 169.254.x.x, DHCP didn't provide an address.

$ Loopback
- Range 127.0.0.0/8 (typically 127.0.0.1 is used)
- Purpose: address that a host uses to talk to itself (test IP stack).
	- packets to 127.0.0.1 never leave the host
? What address can be used to ping the host's own NIC stack? 127.0.0.1

$ Address Classes (historical/classful)
- Class | First octet range | Purpose              | Default Mask
- A     | 1-126             | Large networks       | /8 -> 255.0.0.0
- B     | 128-191           | Medium networks      | /16 -> 255.255.0.0
- C     | 192-223           | Small networks       | /24 -> 255.255.255.0
- D     | 224-239           | Multicast            | N/A
- E     | 240-255           | Experimental/Reserve | N/A

- 127.x.x.x is reserved for loopback (not class A hosts!)
- Classful addressing is largely obsolete as CIDR/VLSM replaced it
? What class is 130.25.10.5? First octet 130 -> Class B

$ Subnetting - Fundamentals
- Goal: divide an IP block into subnets or find network/broadcast/usable addresses.
* Key Concepts
	- Prefix length /n = number of network bits. Host bits = 32-n
	- Number of addresses in a block = 2^(host bits)
	- Usable host addresses (traditional) = 2^(host bits) - 2 (subtract network and broadcast)
            	- Exception/nuance: /31 and /32 are special:
		-  RFC 3021 allows /31 for point-to-point links (2 usable addresses, no broadcast)
		- /32 represents one host route
	- Common shortcut values
		- /24 -> 256 addresses -> 254 usable hosts.
		- /16 -> 65,536 addresses -> 65,534 usable hosts.
		- /8 -> 16,777,216 addresses -> 16,777,214 usable hosts.
		- smaller subnets (/30, /29, /28, etc.) are used for point-to-point or small segments.
* How to find host counts
	- Formula: Host bits = 32-prefix; total addresses =2^(host bits); usable = 2^(host bits) -2
	- /24 host bits = 32-24 = 8
	- /24 total addresses =  2^8 = 256
	- /24 usable hosts = 2^8 - 2 = 254
	- /16 host bits = 32-16 = 16
	- /16 total addresses = 2^16 = 65,536
	- /16 usable addresses = 2^16 - 2 = 65,534

$ Mask Notation and Conversion (CIDR vs dotted decimal)
- CIDR: Classless Inter-domain Routing
- /24 means 24 bits set to 1 in mask
- /26 = 11111111.11111111.11111111.11000000 = 255.255.255.192
- Block size method (fast subnetting)
	- For the octet that contains the subnet boundary, block size = 256 - (mask value in that octet)
		- /26 = 255.255.255.192
		- 256-192 = 64, so subnets start every 64 in the octet: .0, .64, .128, .192

$ Calculating Network, Broadcast, and Usable Range - step by step example.
? Given IP 192.168.1.130/26, find the network, broadcast, and usable host range.
	- Step 1: Identify mask and block size
		- /26 = mask 255.255.255.192, block size 64, subnets .0, .64, .128, .192
	- Step 2: Find which block contains 130
		- It's included in .128, so the network address = 192.168.1.128
	- Step 3: Broadcast address
		- The block runs .128 to .191, so the broadcast address = 192.168.1.191
	- Step 4: Usable hosts
		- First usable = network + 1 = 192.168.1.129
		- Last usable = broadcast - 1 = 192.168.1.190
		- Count hosts: /26 has 2^6 = 64 addresses -> 64 - 2 = 62 usable hosts (double check 129...190 inclusive is 62 hosts)

$ VLSM (Variable Length Subnet Mask) - Practical allocation (done properly)
- What it is: Use different-size subnets inside the same network space to save addresses - allocate biggest subnets first.
- Worked VLSM example (step-by-step)
? You have 192.168.10.0/24 and need subnets for: 100 hosts, 50 hosts, 10 hosts, and 2 hosts. Allocate using VLSM
	- Step 1 - sort by largest need: 100, 50, 10, 2
	- Step 2 - Find smallest prefix that fits each need (remember usable = 2^(host bits) - 2)
		- Need 100 hosts -> try /25 (usable 126)
		- host bits = 7 -> 2^7 = 128; usable = 128-2 = 126;
		- Need 50 hosts -> /26 = 62 usable
		- Need 10 hosts -> /28 = 14 usable
		- Need 2 hosts -> /30 = 2 usable
	- Step 3 - Allocate all from the start of the /24
		- Start: 192.168.10.0/24
		- Subnet 1: 192.168.10.0/25; block size 128; usable 192.168.10.1 to 192.168.10.126; broadcast .127;
		- Subnet 2: 192.168.10.128/26; block size 64; usable 192.168.10.129 to .190; broadcast .191;
		- Subnet 3: 192.168.10.192/28; block size 16; usable 192.168.10.193 to .206; broadcast .207;
		- Subnet 4: 192.168.10.208/30; block size 4; usable 192.168.10.209 to .210; broadcast .211;

$ CIDR (Classless Inter-Domain Routing)
- Uses prefixes /n rather than classful A/B/C. 
- Allows for flexible allocation and route aggregation (supernetting)
- Example of aggregation: two /24s - 192.168.0.0/24 and 192.168.1.0/24 can be summarized as 192.168.0.0/23
	- Why? Because /23 has 9 host bits (32-23=9) -> 2^9 = 512 addresses = two /24s (2x256 = 512)
- CIDR can be used to reduce the number of routes in routing tables (route summarization)

$ Multicast, Broadcast, and Special Addresses
- Multicast 224.0.0.0 - 239.255.255.255 (Class D). Used for one-to-many delivery (streaming, routing protocols)
- Limited broadcast: 255.255.255.255 is used to broadcast on local network (not forwarded by routers)
- Directed broadcast: e.g., for network 192.168.1.0/24 directed broadcast = 192.168.1.255 - historically forwarded if router is configured, but often blocked
- 0.0.0.0: "this network" or default route contexts (used in boot/DHCP)
- 169.254.0.0: APIPA





