<Routing Technologies>
*** NOTES ***
$ Static vs. Dynamic Routing
- Static Routing
	- Manually configured by an administrator
	- Router has a fixed path
	- Pros
		- Simple for small networks
		- More secure (not advertised)
		- No overhead
	- Cons
		- Doesn't adapt to network changes
		- Admin burden in large networks
- Dynamic Routing
	- Routers automatically learn new routes via routing protocol
	- Pros
		- Adapts to network failures
		- Reduces admin overhead
	- Cons
		- More CPU/Memory use
		- Less secure if not authenticated
* If scenario says "large, scalable, adaptive network" -> Dynamic

$ Routing Protocols
- BGP (Border Gateway Protocol)
	- Type: Exterior Gateway Protocol (EGP) -> runs between ISPs / ASes
	- Used for Internet routing (between Autonomous Systems)
	- Path-vector protocol (uses AS-path, policies)
	- Very scalable but complex
	* "routing on the Internet" -> BGP
- EIGRP (Enhanced Interior Gateway Routing Protocol)
	- Cisco proprietary (hybrid protocol)
	- Type: Interior Gateway Protocol (IGP) -> within one AS
	- Uses DUAL algorithm (fast convergence)
	* Only works on Cisco; "Cisco proprietary, hybrid" -> EIGRP
- OSPF (Open Shortest Path First)
	- Type: Link-state IGP
	- Uses Dijkstra's algorithm to computer shortest path
	- Converges quickly, hierarchal (areas)
	* "open standard, fast convergence, large enterprise" -> OSPF

$ Route Selection
- Routers use a routing table and choose routes based on administrative distance (AD) and metrics
- Administrative Distance (AD): Trustworthiness of a route
	- Connected = 0 (most trusted)
	- Static = 1
	- EIGRP = 90
	- OSPF = 110
	- RIP = 120
	- External BGP = 20
- Metrics: Cost, hop count, bandwidth, delay
* If multiple routes exist: Lowest AD wins. If AD ties: lowest metric wins

$ NAT (Network Address Translation)
- Translates private IPs to public IPs
- Conserves IPv4 addresses
- Types:
	- Static NAT: 1:1 mapping (one private to one public)
	- Dynamic NAT: uses pool of public IPs
	- PAT: Port Address Translation: Many private IPs share one public IP (adds port numbers)
- PAT = "NAT Overload" -> Most common
	- Example: A home network with many devices but one ISP IP

$ FHRP - First Hop Redundancy Protocol
- Ensures default gateway redundancy
- Without FHRP, if your gateway router fails -> whole network loses internet
- Types:
	- HSRP (Hot Standby Router Protocol) - Cisco Proprietary
	- VRRP (Virtual Router Redundancy Protocol) - Open standard
	- GLBP (Gateway Load Balancing Protocol) - Cisco, adds load balancing
- Virtual IP (VIP): Clients use this as their default gateway. If active router dies, backup takes over seamlessly
* "Redundant default gateway" -> FHRP

$ VIP - Virtual IP Address
- A shared IP address used by multiple devices (load balancers, FHRPs)
- Clients think they're connecting to a single IP, but behind the scenes multiple routers/servers share responsibility
- Example
	- HSRP = Gateway VIP
	- Load balancer VIP = Single IP front-end for multiple web servers

$ Subinterfaces
- Logical interfaces created on a single physical interface
- Commonly used in router-on-a-stick setups
	- One physical router interface handles multiple VLANs by tagging traffic with 802.1q VLAN IDs
- Example:
	Router g0/1.10 = VLAN 10
	Router g0/1.20 = VLAN 20
* "one router interface handling multiple VLANs" -> Sub-interfaces


<Switching Technologies>
*** NOTES ***
$ VLANs - Virtual Local Area Networks
- Definition: Logically separate a physical network into multiple broadcast domains
- Purpose: Improve security, performance, and management
- Key points:
	- Devices in the same VLAN can communicate directly (Layer 2)
	- Devices in different VLANs require a Layer 3 device (router or L3 switch)
	- VLAN IDs: 1-4094 (0 and 4095 reserved)
	- Default VLAN: 1 (management)
- Types:
	- Data VLAN: User Traffic
	- Native VLAN: Untagged traffic on a trunk link (default = VLAN 1)
	- Voice VLAN: Optimized for VoIP
* "Separate broadcast domains on a switch" -> VLANs
* VLAN -> "Virtual Lanes" -> Separate traffic lanes on the same road

$ Interface Configuration
- Access Port: Belongs to one VLAN only. Example: workstation port
- Example:
	- switchport mode access
	- switchport access vlan 10
- Trunk Port: Carries multiple VLANs using 802.1Q tagging. Example: switch-to-switch or switch-to-router
- Example:
	- switchport mode trunk
	- switchport trunk allowed vlan 10,20,30
- Native VLAN: Traffic without tags -> goes to native VLAN
* If a host can't reach another host in the same VLAN -> check access port config
* If VLANs can't traverse switches -> check trunk config

$ Spanning Tree Protocol (STP)
- Definition: Prevents Layer 2 loops (which cause broadcast storms)
- How it works:
	- Elects a root bridge (lowest bridge ID = priority + MAC)
	- Blocks redundant paths, leaves one active path
	- Uses BPDU (Bridge Protocol Data Units) to detect loops
- Variants
	- STP (802.1D) - original, ~50s convergence
	- RSTP (802.1w) - rapid version, faster convergence (~10s)
	- MSTP (802.1s) - Multiple spanning tree instances

$ MTU - Maximum Transmission Unit
- Definition: Largest packet size (in bytes) a network link can carry without fragmentation
- Default: 1500 bytes for Ethernet
- Issues
	- If MTU is too small -> Fragmentation
	- If too big -> packets get dropped (especially in VPN tunnels)
* "Path MTU Discovery" -> process to find optimal MTU between endpoints

$ Jumbo Frames
- Definition: Ethernet frames larger than 1500 bytes (commonly up to 9000 bytes)
- Purpose:
	- Reduces CPU overhead for large data transfers
	- Great for storage networks (SANs) and high-performance computing
- Requirements: Must be supported end-to-end (all switches, NICs, devices)
* "large file transfers, SAN, virtualization" -> Jumbo Frames

<Wireless Devices>
*** NOTES ***
$ Channels
- Definition: Subdivisions of a frequency band that carry wireless signals
- 2.4 GHz band
	- Channels 1-11 (U.S) -> but only 1, 6, 11 are non-overlapping
	- More interference (microwaves, Bluetooth)
- 5 GHz:
	- Better speed, shorter range, less interference
- 6 GHz (Wi-Fi 6E)
	- Even faster, lowest interference, but smallest coverage area
* "Longer range, penetration" -> 2.4 GHz
* "High speed, less interference" -> 5/6 GHz

$ Frequency Options
- 2.4 GHz
	- Better range, worse speed, more interference
- 5 GHz:
	- Better speed, shorter range, less interference
- 6 GHz (Wi-Fi 6E)
	- Even faster, lowest interference, smallest coverage area

$ SSID - Service Set Identifier
- Definition: Name of a wireless network (e.g, "Starbucks_WiFi")
- Key Notes:
	- Can be broadcasted or hidden
	- Hiding SSID != security (easy to sniff)
* "user can't see the Wi-Fi network" -> SSID broadcast is disabled

$ Wireless Network Types
- Ad Hoc: Peer-to-peer, no access point
- Infrastructure Mode: Uses an AP -> standard for enterprises
- Mesh: Multiple APs connected wirelessly -> extends coverage
- Hotspot: Public access point, often unsecured
* "enterprise Wi-Fi" -> infrastructure mode

$ Encryption Standards
- WEP (Wired Equivalent Privacy)
	- Old, broken, insecure (don't use)
- WPA (Wi-Fi Protected Access
	- TKIP encryption, better, but weak today
- WPA2:
	- Uses AES-CCMP, strong, standard for many years
- WPA3:
	- Strongest, uses SAE (Simultaneous Authentication of Equals)
* "most secure today" -> WPA3
* "Best legacy" = WPA2 with AES

$ Guest Networks:
- Definition: Separate network for visitors with limited access
- Purpose: Protects internal resources
- How it works: VLAN separation or captive portal
* "allow internet but no access to internal servers" -> Guest Network

$ Authentication Methods
- Open: No password (public hotspot)
- Pre-Shared Key (PSK): WPA2/WPA3 with shared password
- Enterprise (802.1X with RADIUS)
	- Each user has unique credentials
	- Strongest authentication model
- Captive portal: Redirects to login page (hotels, airports)
* "Coffee shop login page" -> Captive Portal
* "Enterprise Wi-Fi, unique logins" -> WPA2/WPA3 Enterprise with 802.1X

$ Antennas
- Omnidirectional:
	- Radiates 360 degs -> good for general coverage
- Directional:
	- Focused bream -> longer distance, point-to-point links
	- Example: Yagi, parabolic dish
- MIMO (Multiple Input, Multiple Output)
	- Uses multiple antennas for higher throughput
- MU-MIMO (Multi-User MIMO)
	- Serves multiple devices at once
* "Extend Wi-Fi across a parking log" -> Directional
* "Cover office floor" -> Omnidirectional

$ Access Points (APs)
- Definition: Device that allows wireless clients to join a wired network
- Types:
	- Standalone AP: Manually configured
	- Controller-Based AP (Lightweight): Centrally managed by a WLC (Wireless LAN Controller)
	- Cloud-managed APs: managed via vendor cloud (Meraki, Aruba)
- Key Features:
	- SSID broadcast
	- Channel and frequency selection
	- Secuirty settings (WPA2/WPA3)
* "Enterprise with multiple APs managed centrally" -> Controller-based


<Physical Installations>
*** NOTES ***
$ Installation Implications
- Cable Length Limits
	- Cat5e/Cat6/Cat6a -> 100 meters max (90m horizontal + 10m patch)
	- Fiber -> Much longer (depends on mm vs. sm)
	- Exceeding limits = attenuation (signal loss)
- Bend Radius
	- Copper/fiber cables have a minimum bend radius (don't bend too tight -> signal distortion)
- Interference
	- Keep copper away from fluorescent lights, motors, EMI sources
	- Use STP if interference is unavoidable
- Pathways
	- Use cable trays, raceways, plenum spaces (fire-safe rated)
- Labeling & documentation
	- Every cable drop should be labeled for troubleshooting

$ Power Considerations
- Power over Ethernet (PoE)
	- IEEE 802.3af (15.4w)
	- IEEE 802.3at (30W)
	- IEEE 802.3bt (60-100w)
- UPS (Uninterruptible Power Supply)
	- Provides backup during power outages, prevents brownouts/spikes
- RPS (Redundant Power Supply)
	- Critical devices (switches, servers) should have dual PSUs
- Power Budgeting
	- Ensure switch PoE capacity matches all connected devices
- Grounding
	- Prevents electrical hazards and equipment damage

$ Environmental Factors 
- Rack Layout
	- Follow rack unit (RU) standards (1U = 1.75")
	- Heavy devices at the bottom, lighter at the top
- Cooling
	- Hot aisle/cold aisle design -> airflow management
	- Never block vents
- Temperature & Humidity
	- Optimal: 64-81 deg F (18-27C)
	- Humidity: ~40-60% to prevent ESD (electrostatic discharge) or condensation
- Fire Suppression
	- FM-200, Halon, or CO2 for data centers (avoid water)
- Cable Management
	- Patch panels, velcro ties (not zip ties, which crush cables)
- Physical Security
	- Locked racks, restricted access, CCTV

