<Documentation>
*** NOTES ***
$ Physical vs. Logical Diagrams
- Physical Diagram
	- Definition: Shows the actual physical layout of the network - where devices, cables, and racks are located
	- Purpose: Used by technicians to locate and maintain equipment
	- Includes:
		- Device models, serial numbers, rack locations
		- Cable types and connections	
		- Physical ports and wiring closets	
		- Power supplies and UPS units
	- Tools: Visio, Lucidchart, NetBox, manual rack elevation diagrams
* "A technician needs to locate equipment" -> Physical Diagram
- Logical Diagram
	- Definition: Shows how data flows through the network - subnets, VLANs, IP addressing, routing relationships
	- Purpose: Used by network engineers to understand topology and routing
	- Includes:
		- IP address ranges
		- VLAN IDs and trunk links
		- Routing protocols (OSPF, BGP)
		- Firewalls and logical segmentation
		- WAN links and VPN tunnels
* "A network engineer needs to troubleshoot communication paths" -> Logical Diagram

$ Rack Diagrams
- Definition: A front-view diagram of a server/network rack, showing the placement of equipment (measured in RU (Rack Units))
- Purpose: Helps plan physical installations and cooling
- Details
	- 1U = 1.75 inches
	- Devices labeled by RU position (Ex: Switch 2U-3U)
	- Includes UPS, patch panels, cable management bars
	- Heavy devices (UPS, servers) at bottom, lighter at top

$ Cable Maps
- Definition: Diagrams or spreadsheets that document how every port and cable in the network connects
- Purpose: Aids troubleshooting and moves/adds/changes (MACs)
- Details:
	- Include: Cable ID, type, source/destination, patch panel port, length
	- Example: Cable #C-015 -> Patch Panel A-Port 8 -> Switch 1 - Port 12
	- Often used with labeling systems and cable testers
* "To trace an unknown cable" -> Check cable map

$ Network Diagrams
- Definition: Broad visual representation of the entire topology, combining physical and logical views
- Purpose: Provides overview of all network connections, locations, and devices
- Includes:
	- WAN/LAN boundaries
	- Routers, switches, firewalls, servers, ISPs
	- IP ranges and VLANs
- Tool: Microsoft Visio, Draw.io, NetBrain
* "Engineer wants to visualize all routers and subnets" -> network diagram		

$ Asset Inventory
- Definition: Comprehensive list of all hardware and software assets in the network
- Purpose: Tracks lifecycle, warranty, and security posture
- Details:
	- Hardware: Serial number, model, location, owner
	- Software: OS, license keys, firmware version
	- Include peripheral devices and IoT nodes
- Automation Tools: Lansweeper, SolarWinds, ManageEngine Asset Explorer
* "Identify outdated firmware or missing device" -> consult asset inventory

$ IP Address Management (IPAM)
- Definition: The centralized system for tracking and managing IP addresses, subnets, and DNS/DHCP records
- Purpose: Prevent IP conflicts, manage DHCP scopes, track utilization
- Functions:
	- Manage IPv4/IPv6 assignments
	- Integrate with DNS and DHCP servers
	- Show which addresses are available/used
- Tools: Infoblox, phpIPAM, BlueCat
* "Prevent duplicate IP assignment" -> Use IPAM

$ Service Level Agreement (SLA)
- Definition: A contract between a service provider and customer, defining expected performance and responsibilities
- Purpose: Set expectations for uptime, response time, and penalties
- Common Metrics: 
	- Uptime/Availability: e.g., "99.9% uptime" = ~43.8 mins downtime/month
	- Response/Resolution Time: e.g., "Response within 1 hour for Priority 1 incidents?
	- Throughput/Latency guarantees for WAN or cloud services.
- Monitoring: Network admins use tools to verify SLA compliance
* "agreement defines uptime and response time" -> SLA

$ Wireless Surveys
- Passive Site Survey
	- Listen-only mode - maps existing Wi-Fi coverage, interference, and signal strength
	- Tool: Ekahau, NetSpot, AirMagnet
	- Used for troubleshooting and optimization
- Active Site Survey
	- Client connects to APs and measures performance (throughput, latency)
	- Used to validate new installations or upgrades
- Predictive Survey
	- Simulated design based on floor plans and materials - used before deployment
	- Uses algorithms to predict coverage based on wall types, AP power, antenna patterns
- Survey Goals
	- Identify dead zones, interference, and optimal AP placement
	- Ensure coverage overlaps 10-15% between cells
	- Avoid co-channel interference on same frequency
* "Determine best AP placement before installing hardware" -> Predictive survey
* "Identify dead zones in an existing network" -> Passive Survey



<Life-cycle Management>
*** NOTES ***
$ What it is
- Definition: The process of taking, maintaining and replacing network hardware and software from initial deployment until final retirement.
- Goal: Keep the network secure, reliable, and compliant by ensuring outdated or unsupported devices and software are replaced and upgraded.
- Phases:
	- Planning - Choosing compatible hardware/software
	- Procurement - Purchasing and documenting assets
	- Deployment - Installing and configuring
	- Maintenance - Updating,, patching, monitoring
	- EOL/EOS - Decommission and replace

$ End-of-Life (EOL)
- Definition: Means the vendor has stopped selling or manufacturing a product
- Implications:
	- No new purchase or hardware replacements available.
	- Limited warranty or parts availability
	- Vendor recommends upgrading to newer model

$ End-of-Support
- Definition:
	- The vendor no longer provides technical support, security patches, or updates
- Implications:
	- No bug/security fixes
	- Incompatibly with newer hardware/software
	- Increased operational and security risk
? 'a network device is still functional but receives no updates -> End of Support

$ Software Management
- Software/Firmware updates
	- Keep firmware up to date to fix bugs and vulnerabilities
	- Plan updates during maintenance windows
	- Use centralized management tools 
	- Maintain version control
- Patch Management
	- Schedule regular patches
	- Apply security patches first, then feature updates
	- Always test patches in a lab or staging environment before production
	- Document all updates
- Change Management
	- Formal process for applying software updates or configuration changes
	- Steps: Request -> Review -> Approve -> Test -> Implement -> Document -> Review
	- Reduces risk of outages or misconfigs.
- Configuration Management
	- Maintain consistent configurations using automation tools (Ansible, Puppet, Chef)
	- Use backups and version control for router/switch configs.
- Software Licensing
	- Track license expirations, seat counts, and usage
	- Noncompliance can cause legal or operational issues.

$ Decommissioning
- Definition: The controlled retirement and removal of hardware for software that is no longer secure, supported, or needed
- Steps to proper decommissioning:
	- Plan the Decommissioning
		- Verify the device's replacement is operational
		- Schedule during low-impact periods
		- Notify affected users/departments
	- Data Sanitization
		- Wipe all storage (hard drives, flash memory) using secure methods
		- Use NIST SP 800-88 guidelines for data destruction (overwrite, degauss, shred)
		- Critical to prevent data leaks
	- Remove from Network:
		- Delete IP/DNS entries
		- Remove from authentication systems (RADIUS, AD)
		- Update documentation (asset inventory, diagrams, IPAM)
	- Disposal or Recycling
		- Use certified e-waste vendors or return to manufacturer
		- Follow environmental and compliance standards
	- Post-Decommission Documentation:
		- Update asset inventory (mark device as retired)
		- Record reason for removal and replacement details


<Change Management>
*** NOTES ***
$ What it is
- Definition
	- Change management is the structured process of planning, approving, implementing, and documenting changes
		to a networks infrastructure or configuration.
- Purpose:
	- To ensure every change is:
		- Safe
		- Controlled
		- Documented
		- Reversible

$ The Change Management Lifecycle
- Step 1: Change Request (RFC - Request for Change)
	- Definition: A formal request submitted to propose a network change
	- Includes:
		- Description of the change
		- Business justification
		- Devices/services affected
		- Risk assessment
		- Rollback plan
		- Requested day/time window
		- Responsible parties
	
? 'Technician must document potential impacts and rollback plan" -> Change Request

- Step 2: Review and Approval
	- The Change Advisory Board (CAB) or management team reviews the request
	- They assess:
		- Risk vs. Reward
		- Impact on business operations
		- Scheduling (maintenance window suitability)
		- Rollback and testing plans
	- Only after approval can the change proceed
? 'Who authorizes the change?' -> Change Advisory Board (CAB)

- Step 3: Planning and Testing
	- Changes are tested in a lab/staging environment first
	- Testing verifies:
		- Functionality
		- Compatibility
		- Rollback procedure works
	- Change Plan Should Include:
		- Step-by-step implementation procedure
		- Verification checklist
		- Rollback steps
		- Responsible technicians
? 'Technician applied change without testing; network went down' -> Change Management Process

- Step 4: Implementation
	- The actual deployment of the approved change
	- Typically occurs during maintenance windows (off-peak hours)
	- Admins follow the approved change plan exactly - no improvisation
	- All actions are logged in real-time
? "When is the change actually made?" -> Implementation Phase

- Step 5: Verification / Validation
	- Confirm the change achieved the intended outcome
	- Test all affected services to ensure they're operational
	- Document results and any deviations from the plan
? "The technician confirms network performance after modification" -> Verification Stage

- Step 6: Documentation and Tracking
	- Update:
		- Network diagrams
		- Asset inventory
		- IPAM records
		- Configuration management database (CMDB)
	- Record:
		- Who made the change
		- When and why was it made
		- Results and issues encountered
	- Tracking Tools:
		- ITSM systems (ServiceNow, Jira Service Management, SolarWinds service Desk)
		- CMDB (Configuration Management Database)
? "If a switch was replaced but asset records weren't updated" -> failure in change tracking/documentation

- Step 7: Post-Implementation Review
	- Team evaluates:
		- Was the change Successful?
		- Were there unexpected issues?
		- Lessons learned for next time
	- CAB signs off and closes the request
? "Meeting after change implementation to review success" -> Post-Implementation review


<Configuration Management>
*** NOTES ***
$ What it is:
	- Definition: Configuration Management (CM) is the systematic process of maintaining consistent settings and records 
		for network devices and systems - routers, switches, firewalls, servers, etc.
	- Goal:
		- Ensure devices operate predictably
		- Prevent configuration drift (unauthorized or accidental changes)
