$ What is Disaster Recovery (DR)?
- Definition: DR is the process and plan for restoring IT systems and network operations after a major disruption (natural disaster, power failure, cyberattack, data corruption, etc.)
- Goal: Minimize downtime, data loss, and business support
- Related Concepts:
	- Business Continuity (BC): Keeping the business running during and after an incident
	- Disaster Recovery (DR): Restoring systems and data after the incident

$ RPO (Recovery Point Objective)
- Definition: How much data loss the organization can tolerate during a disaster
- Example: If your RPO = 2 hours, that means backups must occur often enough that the business can survive losing up 2 hours of data
? "RPO" -> How much data you can lose

$ RTO (Recovery Time Objective)
- Definition: How long it takes to restore operations after a disaster
- Example: if RTO = 4 hours, your recovery plan must get systems operational within 4 hours after failure
? "RTO" -> How long you can be down

$ MTTR (Mean Time To Repair)
- Definition: Average time it takes to repair or restore a failed component or service
? "MTTR" -> Average repair time

$ MTBF (Mean Time Between Failures)
- Definition: Average time between one failure and the next
? "MTBF" -> Reliability metric, not downtime

$ Cold, Warm, and Hot Sites
- These refer to recovery site readiness levels - alternate locations where business operations can resume
- Cold Site: Empty building or minimal infrastructure: no equipment required, slowest recovery speed, lowest cost
- Warm Site: Has power, network, and some equipment, medium recovery speed, medium cost
- Hot Site: Fully functional, real-time replication with full equipment, fastest recovery speed, highest cost

$ Active-Active vs. Active-Passive
- Active-Active: Both systems are live and share the workload
	- If one fails, the other already has the same data and continues seamlessly
	- Common in data centers, load balancing, and clustered databases.
- Active-Passive: One system is live, the other sites idle
	- Passive node activates only during failover
	- Simpler and cheaper but slower to failover

$ Disaster Recovery Testing
- Purpose: To verify that disaster recovery plans actually work - not just exist on paper
- Types of DR Tests:
	- Checklist / Tabletop: Review DR plan on paper with team discussion
	- Simulation / Walkthrough: Simulated disaster event; staff perform procedures
	- Parallel Test: Secondary systems activated alongside production (wihtout interruption)
	- Full interruption Test: Production systems taken offline to test complete recovery

