$ DHCP (Dynamic Host Configuration Protocol)
- Purpose: Automatically assigns IP addresses and other network configuration parameters (subnet mask, gateway, DNS servers) to hosts
- Protocol: UDP (Ports 67 for server, 68 for client)
- How it Works - The DORA Process
	- D - Discover: Client broadcasts to find DHCP servers; Message Type: Broadcast
	- O - Offer: Server responds with an IP lease offer; Message Type: Broadcast
	- R - Request: Client requests the offered address; Message Type: Broadcast
	- A - Acknowledge: Server confirms lease and sends config info; Message Type: Broadcast/Unicast
- Result: Client receives an IP address, subnet mask, gateway, DNS server, and lease time.
- DHCP Lease Terms:
	- Lease Time: Duration the IP is assigned
	- Reservation: Permanent IP assignment at a specific MAC address
	- Scope: Range of IP addresses available for lease
	- Exclusion Range: IPs within the scope that are not assigned
	- Relay Agent: Forwards DHCP broadcasts across routers (UDP 67/68 -> uses IP Helper Address)
? If a client shows an IP starting with 169.254.x.x, DHCP failed - and APIPA took over.

$ SLAAC (Stateless Address AutoConfiguration)
- Purpose: Automatically configures IPv6 addresses without a DHCPv6 server.
- Key Concept: SLAAC uses ICMPv6 messages and Router Advertisements (RA) to let hosts self-generate their IPv6 address.
- How SLAAC Works:
	- Router Advertisement (RA): Router sends out network prefix info
	- Host Self-Configures: Host appends its own interface ID (usually from MAC address via EUI-64)
	- Optional DNS info: Sent using RDNSS (Recursive DNS Server option) in the RA
- Example:
	- Router Prefix = 2001:db8:acad:1::/64
	- Host MAC = 00-1A-2B-3C-4D-5E
	- Generated IPv6 = 2001:db8:acad:1:21a:2bff:fe3c:4d5e
- Advantages:
	- No DHCP server needed
	- Simple Fast setup
- Disadvantages:
	- Limited control (no centralized lease management)
	- Harder to manage large enterprise networks
? If IPv6 devices get addresses without DHCP, it's SLAAC using ICMPv6 Router Advertisements

$ DNS (Domain Name System)
- Purpose: Translates domain names (like google.com) into IP addresses that computers use to communicate
- Protocol: UDP/TCP port 53

- How DNS Resolution works:
	- Client Query: User enters "example.com" -> DNS request sent to resolver
	- Recursive Resolver: Queries multiple servers on behalf of client
	- Root Server: Directs query to appropriate TLD server (.com, .net, etc.)
	- TLD Server: Points to the authoritative server for "example.com"
	- Authoritative Server: Returns final IP address
	- Caching: Resolver stores the answer (TTL - Time to Live)
- DNS Record Types:
	- A: IPv4 address: example.com -> 93.184.216.34
	- AAAA: IPv6 address: example.com -> 2606:2800:220:1:248:1893:25c8:1946
	- CNAME: Alias for another domain: mail.example.com -> ghs.google.com
	- MX: Mail Server: example.com -> mail.example.com
	- PTR: Reverse lookup (IP -> hostname): 93.184.216.34 -> example.com
	- TXT: Misc info (SPF, DKIM, etc): SPF record for email validation
	- NS: Nameserver delegation: ns1.example.com
- Forward vs Reverse Lookup Zones
	- Forward: Name -> IP
	- Reverse: IP -> Name
? "resolves hostnames to IPs" -> DNS A or AAAA
? " Resolves IPs to hostnames" -> PTR record

$ NTP (Network Time Protocol)
- Purpose: Synchronizes the clocks of network devices to a reference time source
- Protocol: UDP 123
- Why it Matters?:
	- Log correlation and security audits require consistent timestamps
	- Encryption (Kerberos, TLS) relied on time accuracy
- How it Works:
	- Devices form a hierarchical system of 'strata'
		- Stratum 0: Atomic clock or GPS reference (satellite)
		- Stratum 1: Server directly connected to Stratum 0 (time.nist.gov)
		- Stratum 2: Syncs from Stratum 1, and so on (your router)
	- Offset: Difference between system time and reference time
? Which protocol ensures all devices have synchronized timestamps? -> NTP

$ PTP (Precision Time Protocol)
- Purpose: Provides high-accuracy time synchronization, better than NTP, for environments that need sub-microsecond precision. (financial networks, telco, etc.)
- Defined in: IEEE 1588
- How it Works:
	- Uses a master-slave clock model
	- Syncs time using hardware time stamping (NIC or switch-level)
	- Accounts for network delay asymmetry, unlike NTP
	- Common in Ethernet and industrial control systems

$ NTS (Network Time Security)
- Purpose: Provides cryptographic authentication and integrity for NTP messages - preventing spoofing or MITM attacks
- Problem it solves:
	- NTP is plaintext, so attackers could send fake time updates
	- NTS adds TLS encryption and authentication tokens
- How it Works:
	- NTP client connects to NTS-enabled server using NTS-KE (Key Establishment) over TLS (port 4460)
	- Secure session keys are exchanged
	- Future NTP packets use those keys for integrity verification (UDP 123)
- Key Features:
	- Protects against time-spoofing attacks
	- Backward-compatible with NTP
	- Standardized in RFC 8915
? "Which protocol secures NTP communications using encryption and authentication?" -> NTS

