<Deception Technologies>
*** NOTES ***
- What they are:
	- Honeypot: Single decoy system or service configured to look attractive to attackers (e.g., an SSH server web app, or IoT device) and instrumented to log and alert on attacker activity
	- Honeynet: A network of one or more honeypots (often multiple systems and services) designed to emulate an entire environment so attackers interact with a realistic network segment
- Goals/Values
	- Early detection of intrusions (low noise/high signal)
	- Intelligence gathering: attacker tools, tactics, IOCs, malware samples
	- Delay and Containment: occupy attackers away from production
	- Support forensics, threat hunting, and signatures for prevention tooling
- Types of Honeypots (interaction level)
	- Low-Interaction
		- Emulates limited services (fake SSH banner, fake ports)
		- Pros: safe, low-resource, easy to deploy, low risk of compromise
		- Cons: Limited intelligence (attacker often probes then moves on)
	- High-Interaction
		- Real OS/services that allow attackers to fully interact (real shell, file system)
		- Pros: rich telemetry (full TTP capture)
		- Cons: higher risk must isolate strongly, higher maintenance, legal/ethical considerations
	- Medium-interaction
		- Somewhere in between (more believable than low-interaction, but contained)
- Honeynet Architectures / Deployment models
	- Standalone honeypot: single system on isolated VLAN, monitored and logged
	- Honeynet (lab-style): Multiple hosts (web server, DB, AD-like mock), routers/switches, simulated services to emulate an enterprise
	- Production-deceptive overlays: deception services deployed inline or alongside production (e.g., decoy credentials, fake shares)
	- Cloud-based honeypots: cloud instances that emulate services(Useful for internet-exposed threat intelligence)
- Isolation and safety (critical)
	- Put honeypots on segmented networks (dedicated VLANs, no route to production)
	- Use egress filtering (drop or tightly control outgoing traffic) to prevent a compromised honeypot from being used to attack others
	- Use visualization and snapshots for fast rollback
	- Monitor hypervisor/host integrity = keep the host fully secured and separate
	- Prefer low-interaction if you can't guarantee strict isolation
- Instrumentation and Telemetry
	- Full packet capture (.pcap) of inbound/outbound traffic
	- System call logging, keystroke/session capture for high-interaction
	- File and binary collection (quarantine malware samples)
	- Time-stamped logs forwarded to SIEM / central log aggregator
	- Alerts on rare or suspicious events (e.g., unusual persistence attempts)
	- Integration: Tie honeypot events into SIEM, IDS/IPS, threat intelligence feeds, and automated response playbooks
- Common honeypot software and frameworks (examples)
	- Cowrie - SSH/telnet medium-interaction honeypot (captures sessions, credentials)
	- Dionaea - malware-capturing honeypot (Windows-exploits focus)
	- Honeyd - low-interaction virtual host emulator
	- MHN (Modern Honey Network) / Cuckoo + MHN - central management for many honeypots
	- Canarytokens/Thinkst Canary - lightweight "tripwires" (documents, DNS tokens) that alert when touched
- Use cases and examples
	- Credential harvesting: collect username/password attempts against fake SSH or web login
	- Malware capture: expose vulnerable protocols and capture binaries for analysis
	- Attack profiling: observe lateral movement techniques in a controlled network
	- False-target diversion: distract an intruder while defenders response
	- Testing IDS/alerting: validate detection rules with real malicious activity
- What to monitor and common indicators of compromise (IOCs)
	- Unexpected login attempts, brute-force patterns (many SSH username/password combos)
	- New outbound connections to C2 domains from the honeypot
	- File writes/changes to binaries or startup items
	- Exploit attempts (specific exploit strings, usual packets)
	- Uncommon processes spawned or shell commands executed
- Forensics and Analysis
	- Capture full .pcap and disk snapshots
	- Extract malware artifacts for sandboxing (Cuckoo) and static/dynamic analysis
	- Correlate honeypot IOCs with enterprise telemetry to hunt for lateral or pivoting activity
- Legal, Ethical and Policy Considerations
	- Privacy: don't intentionally capture unrelated third-party personal data
	- Entrapment concerns: honeypots are defensive; don't craft content that entices or coerces users illegally
	- Outbound abuse: prevent your honeypot being used to attack others - implement egress controls and legal disclaimers where required
	- Lawful monitoring: follow organizational policy and local laws when collecting data
- Pitfalls and Limitations:
	- False positives: scanners and benign crawlers may hit your honeypot - classify them
	- Maintenance overhead: especially for high-interaction honeypots needing snapshots, patching analysis
	- Advanced adversaries: skilled attackers can detect honeypots (fingerprinting) and avoid them or feed false data
	- Resource cost: capturing and analyzing large volumes of data takes compute and analyst time
- Best Practices (quick checklist)
	1. Isolate: dedicated VLAN , strict ACLs, no direct access to prod
	2. Limit egress: prevent honeypot from being a launchpad for attacks
	3. Centralize logs: SIEM integration and timestamp synchronization (NTP)
	4. Use layered deception: combine low- and high-interaction depending on need
	5. Legal review: ensure compliance and written authorization
	6. Rotate snapshots: rollback corrupted honeypots quickly
	7. Correlate intelligence: feed IOCs to firewalls/IDS and update signatures
	8. Test for detectability: use red-team to see fi honeypots get fingerprinted by attackers


<Security Terminology>
*** NOTES ***
- Core Terms Overview
	- Asset - Anything of value to the organization that needs protection (data, systems, hardware, people, reputation) - Company database, routers, customer info - Assets are what you protect
	- Threat - Any potential danger that can exploit a vulnerability to harm an asset - Hacker, malware, natural, disaster, insider - threats exploit vulnerabilities
	- Vulnerability - A weakness or flaw that could be exploited - Unpatched OS, weak passwords, open ports, misconfigured ACLs - Vulnerabilities are what threats use
	- Exploit - The action or method used by a threat actor to take advantage of a vulnerability - Using an SMB exploit to gain admin access - Exploits are what turn vulnerabilities into attacks
	- Risk - The likelihood of a threat exploiting a vulnerability combined with the potential impact - The change that ransomware encrypts your data and halts business operations - Risk = Threat x Vulnerability x Impact
- The CIA Triad
	- Element - Meaning - Example Threat - Common Protections
	- Confidentiality - Keeping data secret from unauthorized users - Data breach, eavesdropping, password theft, Encryption, MFA, access control lists (ACLs)
	- Integrity - Ensuring data hasn't been tampered with or altered - Man-in-the-middle altering data, malware modifying files - Hashing, digital signatures, file permissions, version control
	- Availability - Ensuring systems and data are accessible when needed - DoS/DDoS attack, power outage, hardware failure - Redundancy, backups, load balancing UPS power
- Risk Management Concepts
	- Term - Definition - Example
	- Risk Assessment - Process of identifying and prioritizing risks - Ranking servers by how critical they are
	- Risk Mitigation - Implementing controls to reduce risk - Installing firewalls, patching systems
	- Risk Acceptance - Choosing to accept a risk (cost < impact) - Not encrypting non-sensitive logs
	- Risk Avoidance - Eliminating the activity causing the risk - Shutting down a vulnerable system
	- Risk Transference - Shifting risk to another entity - Buying cyber insurance or outsourcing services


<Audits and Compliance>
*** NOTES ***
- Compliance = meeting the legal, regulatory, and policy requirements that apply to your organization
	- You must:
		- Handle and store data securely
		- Maintain audio trails of access, configurations, and incidents
		- Follow industry frameworks (like PCI DSS or GDPR)
		- Prove compliance through audits (internal and external)
- Key Concepts
	- Term - Definition - Example
	- Audit - A formal review of systems, configurations, and processes to ensure compliance with standards and policies - External auditor reviewing firewall configurations for PCI DSS
	- Data Locality - The physical or geographic location where data is stored or processed - Customer data stored in EU data centers due to GDPR
	- Compliance Frameworks - Industry standards or laws that set rules for security and privacy - PCI DSS, GDPR, HIPPA, ISO 23001

$ Data Locality
	- Definition: The physical location of where data is stored, transmitted, or processed - especially relevant in cloud environments and multi-region architectures
	- Why is matters:
		- Different countries and regions have different data protection laws
		- Some regulations prohibit data from leaving a geographic region (known as data sovereignty)
		- Organizations must ensure that sensitive data stays within allows jurisdictions
	- Examples:
		- GDPR: personal data of EU citizens must stay within the EU or be transferred only to countries with adequate protection
		- US Government Contracts: May require data to be hosted in FedRAMP-approved US data centers
		- Cloud deployments: AWS/Azure/GCP regions are often chosen based on data locality requirements
	? "data stored in a different region" or "data sovereignty violation" -> Data locality

$ PCI DSS - Payment Card Industry Data Security Standard
	- Definition: A security standard created by major credit card companies to protect cardholder data
	- Applies to: any organization that stores, processes, or transmits credit/debit card data
	- Core Principles:
		- Cardholder Data Environment (CDE): The systems and networks that store, process, or transmit payment data - must be strictly isolated
		- Encryption: Card data must be encrypted using TLS during transmission and AES or similar at rest
		- Logging: Access and system changes must be logged and reviewed regularly
		- Audits: Regular compliance audits by a Qualified Security Assessor (QSA)
	? "encrypting customer payment data", "A company is fined for storing unencrypted card numbers" -> PCI DSS violation

$ GDPR - General Data Protection Regulation (EU)
	- Definition: An EU regulation that protects the personal data and privacy of individuals in the EU
	- Applies to: Any organization (worldwide) that collects, stores, or processes data on EU citizens, even if the company is not based in the EU
	- Core Principles:
		- Lawfulness, fairness, and transparency -> Organizations must clearly explain what data they collect and why
		- Purpose limitation -> Data collected must only be used for the stated purpose
		- Data minimization -> Collect only what is necessary
		- Accuracy -> Keep data up-to-date and correct
		- Storage limitation -> Retain data only as long as needed
		- Integrity and Confidentiality -> Secure data using appropriate controls (encryption, access control)
		- Accountability -> Organizations must demonstrate compliance (documentation, policies)
	- Key Roles:
		- Data Subject - The individual whose data is being collected
		- Data Controller - The organization that determines why and how personal data is processed
		- Data processor - The third party that processes data on behalf of the controller (e.g., cloud provider)
		- DPO (Data Protection Officer) - Responsible for overseeing compliance and reporting breaches
	- GDPR Rights of Data Subjects
		- Right to be informed -> Users must know how their data is used
		- Right to access -> Users can request a copy of their data
		- Right to rectification -> Correct inaccurate data
		- Right o erasure ("Right to be forgotten") -> Have data deleted
		- Right to restrict processing -> Temporarily stop data use
		- Right to data portability -> Move data to another provider
		- Right to object -> Opt out of processing (like marketing)
		- Rights related to automated decision making -> Avoid unfair algorithmic profiling
	- GDPR Compliance Requirements
		- Encrypt data in transit and at rest
		- Implement data minimization and privacy by design
		- Report breaches within 72 hours to regulators
		- Maintain records of processing activities
		- Ensure cross-border transfers comply with EU standards
		- Penalties: Up to 20 million euros or 4% of global annual turnover, whichever is higher


<Network Segmentation>
*** NOTES ***
- Definition: Dividing a network into smaller, isolated zones (segments) using VLANs, firewalls, or physical separation to improve security, performance, and control
- Goals:
	- Limit the spread of attacks or malware
	- Enforce least privilege between network zones
	- Contain high-risk devices (e.g., IoT) from critical systems
	- Optimize traffic performance
	- Support compliance (e.g., PCI DSS requires segmentation of cardholder stat networks)
- Segmentation Techniques:
	- Method - Description - Example
	VLAN (Virtual Lan) - Logical segmentation within a switch; separates traffic without separate hardware - VLAN 10 = Corp, VLAN 20 = Guest, VLAN 30 = IoT
	Subnetting - Divides IP ranges into isolated networks; can be paired with ACLs - 10.0.10.0/24 for IoT, 10.0.20.0/24 for Finance
	Firewall/Zoning - Uses firewalls to enforce rules between segments - SCADA network only allows Modbus TCP from certain IPs
	Physical Separation - Separate cables, switches, or NICs; used in high-security or OT environments - SCADA and IT systems on separate physical hardware
	Network Access Control (NAC) - Restricts device access based on type, compliance or identity - BYOD devices placed in guest VLAN

$ IoT - Internet of Things
	- Definition: IoT devices are consumer or enterprise devices that connect to networks and share data - often with limited security or no patching ability
	- Examples: Smart TVs, security cameras, smart thermostats, badge readers, sensors
	- Security Risks:
		- Default Credentials
		- Rarely patched firmware
		- Weak encryption or plan HTTP communication
		- Easily hijacked for botnets (like Mirai)
	- Segmentation Approach:
		- Place IoT devices on their own VLAN/subnet
		- Restrict east-west traffic (IoT -> Corp)
		- Allow only outbound access to known cloud endpoints
		- Use firewall rules or ACLs to limit communication
		- Monitor with IDS/IPS and network behavior analysis
$ IIOT - Industrial Internet of Things
	- Definition: A subset of IoT focused on industrial environments, integrating sensors, controllers, and actuators used in manufacturing, energy, or logistics
	- Security Risks:
		- Legacy Protocols with no encryption
		- Weak physical security in remote sites
		- Vendor remote access without MFA
	- Segmentation Approach:
		- Place IIoT devices in isolated industrial VLANs or zones
		- Use firewall ACLs to tightly control communication with enterprise IT
		- Deploy DMZ between IT and OT environments
		- Implement NAC to verify device authenticity

$ SCADA - Supervisory Control and Data Acquisition
	- Definition: SCADA systems monitor and control industrial processes (e.g., power grids, water treatment, pipelines)
	- They collect telemetry and send control commands to remote devices
	- Components:
		- RTUs (Remote Terminal Units) - gather data from sensors
		- PLCs (Programmable Logic Controllers) - execute control logic
		- HMI (Human-Machine Interface) - Operator dashboard
	- Security Risks:
		- SCADA devices often run legacy OS (like windows XP)
		- Flat networks with full connectivity = easy lateral movement
		- No native encryption or authentication in old protocols
	- Segmentation Approach
		- Place SCADA systems on a dedicated, isolated network
		- Use firewalls and DMZs between IT and OT networks
		- Limit remote access with VPN + MFA
		- Use unidirectional gateways (data diodes) where appropriate

$ ICS - Industrial Control System
	- Definition: Broad term that includes SCADA, DCS (Distributed Control Systems), and PLCs - used to manage and automate industrial operations
	- ICS Characteristics:
		- Prioritizes availability and safety over confidentiality
		- Often has real-time operational requirements
		- Runs decade-old systems not designed for today's cybersecurity threats
	- Segmentation Approach:
		- Separate ICS and IT networks
		- Control communication using strict firewalls
		- Monitor with passive intrusion detection (no active scans) to avoid downtime
		- Use jump servers for admin access

$ OT - Operational Technology
	- Definition: Hardware and software that detects or causes changes in physical processes through direct monitoring and control
	- Includes: SCADA, PLCsk, sensors, actuators, and industrial controllers
	- Security Focus:
		- OT devices control critical infrastructure - downtime can cause physical harm
		- Security focus:  availability and integrity > confidentiality
	- Segmentation Approach:
		- Separate OT from IT with firewalls or DMZs
		- Use network zoning (Operations, Control, Enterprise)
		- Apply NAC to prevent rogue devices
		- Implement role-based access control (engineers vs. IT admins)

$ Guest Networks
	- Definition: A separate VLAN/subnet that allows visitors or unmanaged devices to access the internet - but not the internal network
	- Best Practices:
		- Use VLAN isolation and ACLs to prevent guest -> internal access
		- use captive portal authentication (login splash page)
		- Restrict bandwidth and service access (only DNS, HTTP/HTTPS)
		- Monitor and log all guest activity
	? "Employees can connect personal phones to wi-fi but can't access internal servers" -> That's a guest VLAN

$ BYOD - Bring Your Own Device
	- Definition: Policy allowing employees to use personal devices (laptops, phones, tablets) for work
	- Security Risks:
		- Personal devices are outside corporate patch management
		- Risk of data leakage, malware, or unauthorized access
		- Devices may lack MDM (Mobile Device Management) controls
	- Segmentation and Controls:
		- Assign BYOD devices to their own VLAN/Subnet
		- Use NAC to check compliance before access (OS version, antivirus, encryption)
		- Restrict access to internet or limited internal resources
		- Use VPN + MFA for secure connections
		- Enforce MDM and containerization (work/personal separation)


<Types of Attacks>
*** NOTES ***
$ Denial of Service (DoS) / Distributed Denial of Service (DDoS)
	- What it is:
		- DoS: Attacker overwhelms a single target (server, link, service) so it becomes unavailable
		- DDoS: Many compromised hosts (a botnet) flood the target, making the attack larger and harder to stop
	- How it manifests (high level)
		- Flood of traffic (volume), connection exhaustion (stateful resources), or targeted protocol/application abuse
	- Indicators:
		- Sudden spike in traffic/bandwidth utilization
		- Excessive half-open connections or resource exhaustion on servers
		- Legitimate users can't connect; monitoring shows service down even though hardware is up
	- Mitigations / Defenses:
		- Rate limiting, traffic filtering, geo-IP blocks
		- Upstream scrubbing / DDoS protection services (CDNs, cloud scrubbing)
		- Autoscaling for capacity, redundant infrastructure, SYN cookies, ACLs, and proper firewall rules.
		- Blackholding only as last result

$ VLAN Hopping
	- What it is:
		- Attack where a host on one VLAN gains access to traffic on another VLAN it shouldn't reach
	- How it commonly happens (defensive view)
		- Misconfigured trunks (native VLAN mismatches) or use of double-tagging vulnerabilities on older switches
	- Indicators:
		- Devices on a VLAN seeing traffic destined for other VLANs, unauthorized access to VLAN-restricted resources
	- Mitigations / Defenses
		- Disable unused ports, put unused ports in an unused VLAN and shut them down
		- Do not use VLAN 1 for management or native VLAN. Explicitly set native VLANs and avoid VLAN tagging mismatches
		- Use proper trunk configurations, management VLAN separate, port security, and private VLANs if needed

$ MAC Flooding (CAM Table Overflow)
	- What it is:
		- Attacker floods a switch with fake source MAC addresses to overflow the switch's MAC (CAM) table, causing it to behave like a hub and broadcast frames out all ports
	- Indicators: 
		- High broadcast traffic, many MAC addresses in CAM table, legitimate traffic being seen on many ports, unexplained sniffable traffic
	- Mitigations/Defenses:
		- Enable port security (limit number of MACs per port), set static MACs for critical ports
		- Use 802.1X on access ports
		- Implement dynamic ARP inspection and DHCP snooping to complement

$ ARP Poisoning / ARP Spoofing
	- What it is:
		- Attacker forges ARP messages to associate the attacker's MAC with the IP of another host (e.g., the gateway), enabling traffic interception or disruption
	- High-level effect
		- Enables Man--in-the-Middle (MitM) for local networks, can capture or modify traffic or perform DoS
	- Indicators
		- Duplicate MAC/IP entries in ARP table, sudden gateway MAC changes, intermittent connectivity, packet loss, or unexpected traffic flows
	- Mitigations/Defenses
		- Use static ARP entries for critical hosts where practical
		- Enable Dynamic ARP Inspection (DAI) and DHCP snooping on switches
		- Use encrypted protocols (TLS, SSH) to limit information an attacker can exploit even if they intercept traffic
	? "Local hosts can't reach a gateway" or "Mismatched ARP entries" -> Think ARP Spoofing; DAI and DHCP snooping are common answers

$ DNS Poisoning / DNS Spoofing
	- What it is:
		- Attacker corrupts DNS records or responses so users are pointed to malicious IPs (e.g., fake bank site) instead of legitimate sites
	- Common delivery methods (defensive view)
		- Cache poisoning on recursive resolvers, compromised authoritative servers, or intercepted DNS responses (on-path attacks)
	- Indicators:
		- Users are sent to suspicious IPs, many failed TLS certificate checks, DNS responses with odd TTLs or NS records, or unusual DNS query patterns
	- Mitigations / Defenses
		- Use DNSSEC to ensure integrity of DNS records
		- Use reputable recursive resolvers and enforce DNS resolver patching
		- Validate TLS/SSL certificates (HSTS, certificate pinning where appropriate)
		- Monitor DNS logs for anomalies

$ Rogue Devices / Rogue Services
	- What it is:
		- Unauthorized devices or services on the network (e.g., an unauthorized AP, switch, or server) that bypass controls or present risk
	- Examples:
		- Personal access point bridging guest traffic into the corporate LAN; unauthorized HDCP server handing out bad IPs
	- Indicators:
		- New SSIDs, unknown MAC addresses on switch ports, unexpected DHCP offers, or anomalous services in net scans/logs
	- Mitigations / Defenses
		- Network Access Control (NAC) to validate and quarantine unknown devices
		- Monitor with asset inventory and IPAM, use periodic network scans
		- Disable unused ports, use 802.1X, and enforce switch port security
		- For wireless: detect rogue APs with wireless controllers and site surveys
	? "Clients getting wrong IPs" or "Unexpected Wi-Fi SSId in corporate space" -> rogue devices/services

$ Evil Twin (Rogue Wi-Fi / Malicious AP)
	- What it is:
		- Attacker sets up a malicious wireless AP with the same (or similar) SSID as a legitimate network to trick users into connecting, enabling credential capture or traffic interception
	- Indicators:
		- User complaints of captive portals or credential prompts, multiple APS with same SSID, sudden drop in connection security, or certificate/TLS warnings
	- Mitigations/Defenses
		- Educate users to verify the network (avoid auto-joining), use 802.1X (Enterprise Wi-Fi) with strong EAP (no PSK), enforce HTTPS, and use wireless intrusion prevention/detection systems (WIPS/WIDS) to detect rogue APs
		- Use VPN on client devices when on Wi=Fi outside trusted networks

$ On path Attack (Man-in-the-Middle / MitM / On-path)
	- What it is: 
		- Attacker intercepts communications between two parties and can eavesdrop, modify, or replay traffic
	- How it happens (high level)
		- Via ARP spoofing, rogue access points, compromised routers, SSL stripping, or interception at chokepoints
	- Indicators
		- Unexpected TLS certificate warnings, unusual latency, inconsistent content (injected scripts), or authentication failures
	- Mitigations / Defenses
		- Use end-to-end encryption (TLS, SSH), certificate pinning where applicable, HSTS, strong mutual authentication, and secure DNS (DNSSEC + DNS over HTTPS/TLS)
		- Network Segmentation and strict routing policies reduce attacker opportunities
	? "certificate errors while accessing sites" -> MitM

$ Social Engineering
	- Attacks target people rather than systems. They're extremely effective and often the weakest link
	- Common Types
		- Phishing
			- What: Fraudulent emails/webpages that trick users into revealing credentials or running malware
			- Indicators: Suspicious sender address, urgent tone, mismatched URLs, attachments with macros
			- Defenses: User training, email filtering, URL scanning, MFA (so stolen creds alone aren't enough), DMARC/DKIM/SPF
		- Dumpster Diving
			- What: Searching physical trash for sensitive information (passwords, prints, notes)
			- Indicators: Discovery of paper with credentials or config details in trash
			- Defenses: Shredding policies, secure disposal, employee awareness
		- Shoulder Surfing:
			- What: Watching someone enter a password/PIN in public
			- Indicators: Observed snooping near devices or ATMs
			- Defenses: Privacy screens, awareness, shielding keyboards when typing
		- Tailgating (Piggybacking)
			- What: Following authorized personnel into secure areas without authorizatoin
			- Indicators: Unknown person in restricted area, badge access logs without corresponding exit/entry
			- Defenses: Physical access controls, mantraps, security training, enforcement of badge checks, security guards
	

<Security Features and Defense>
*** NOTES ***
$ Device Hardening
	- Definition: Hardening = reducing a device's attack surface by disabling unnecessary services, tightening configurations, and applying security controls
	- Why it matters:
		- Every router, switch, server, or access point ships with default credentials, open ports, and services - easy targets if not secured
	- Key Hardening Steps
		Category                       | Description                                              | Example
		Remove/Disable Unused Services | Reduce attack surface by turning off features not needed | Disable Telnet, FTP, or legacy protocols on network gear
		Patch and Firmware Updates     | Fix vulnerabilities                                      | Regularly update router firmware
		Strong Authentication          | Replace defaults with strong passwords and MFA           | Change admin/admin -> complex credentials, enable 2FA
		Management Interfaces          | Limit access to trusted subnets                          | Only allow SSH from management VLAN
		Encrypt Management Traffic     | Prevent credential theft                                 | Use SSH, HTTPS, SNMPv3, instead of Telnet, HTTP, SNMPv1/2c
		Logging and Monitoring         | Record activity for auditing                             | Enable syslog, SNMP traps
		Physical Security              | Prevent tampering                                        | Lock network cabinets, disable console ports
	? "reduce the number of services exposed on a switch" -> device hardening
	? "disable unused interfaces" -> device hardening

$ NAC - Network Access control
	- Definition: NAC enforces security policies before granting access to the network. It validates devices and uses at connection time
	- Two main types:
		1. Pre-admission NAC: Checks devices before they join the network
		2. Post-admission NAC: Continuously monitors connected devices
	- What NAC Checks:
		- Device posture (antivirus, OS version, encryption)
		- User authentication (credentials, certificates)
		- Device identity (MAC, certificate, profile)
	- Implementation:
		- 802.1X for port-based authentication (switch/AP)
		- RADIUS backend for user/device validation
		- Quarantine VLAN for noncompliant devices
	- Example Flow
		- User plugs into switch port
		- NAC checks endpoint compliance via 802.1X
		- If compliant -> full access VLAN
		- If not -> Quarantined or guest VLAN
	? "devices checked for antivirus or OS version before joining the network" or "quarantined VLAN" -> NAC

$ Key Management
	- Definition: Key Management is the generation, distribution, storage, rotation, and revocation or cryptographic keys used for encryption and authentication
	- Key Lifecycle:
		1. Generation: Securely create keys using strong algorithms (AES, RSA, ECC)
		2. Distribution: Deliver securely (e.g., using PKI or certificates)
		3. Storage: Protect keys (HSMs, TPMs, encrypted storage)
		4. Rotation: Regularly replace keys to reduce exposure time
		5. Revocation: invalidate compromised or expired keys (CRLs, OCSP)
		6. Destruction: securely delete retired keys
	- Types of Keys
		- Symmetric: Same key for encryption/decryption (fast, e.g., AES)
		- Asymmetric: Public/private key pair (RSA, ECC)
		- Session Keys: Temporary symmetric keys for specific sessions
	- Tools:
		- PKI (Public Key Infrastructure) for certificate-based key management
		- HSM (Hardware Security Module) for secure key storage and operations
	? "Centralized certificate management" or "secure key lifecycle" -> Key management

$ ACL - Access Control List
	- Definition: Defines which traffic is allowed or denied through an interface (firewall, router, switch)
	- Core Concept:
		- ACLs filter traffic based on:
			- IP addresses (source/destination)
			- Protocol (TCP, UDP, ICMP)
			- Port numbers
			- Direction (inbound/outbound)
	- ACL Types:
		- Standard ACL -> Filters by source IP only
		- Extended ACL -> Filters by source/destination IP, protocol, and port
		- Named ACL -> Identified by a name instead of number
	- ACL Placement Rules:
		- Standard ACLs -> near destination (simple)
		- Extended ACLs -> near source (efficient)
	- Use Cases:
		- Prevent guest VLAN -> internal VLAN traffic
		- Allow SSH to routers only from admin subnet
		- Block specific IPs or ports
	? "What to use to restrict network traffic based on IP/port" -> ACL

$ URL and Content Filtering
	- Definition: Controls what users can access on the web by blocking or allowing based on URL, keywords, or content category
	- How it works:
		- URL or DNS requests are check against filtering databases (e.g., "gambling", "social media", "malware")
		- Can run on firewalls, proxies, or DNS-level filters
	- Filtering Methods
		Type	Description	Example
		URL Filtering		Matches domain/URL to block list 	 		Block facebook.com or *.xxx
		Content Filtering 	 	Scans page content for forbidden keywords		Block sites with "proxy" or "torrent"
		DNS Filtering		Blocks resolution of known malicious domains	Prevents users from reaching C2 servers
	- Tools:
		- Cloud security gateways (Cisco Umbrella, Zscaler)
		- local proxy servers (Squid, Secure Web Gateway)
	? "company blocks employees from accessing social media during work" or "prevent malware domains" -> URL/content filtering

$ Trust vs. Untrusted Zones
	- Definition: Network zones categorize traffic based on trust level - defining how much control and monitoring each needs
	- Common Zones
		Zone	Description	Example
		Trusted	Internal network, controlled and managed	Corporate LAN, internal servers
		Untrusted	External network, like the Internet		Public WAN
		DMZ 		Buffer zone between trusted and untrusted	Public-facing web or email servers
		Management 	Restricted access for admins only 		Router/switch management subnet
		Guest		Internet-only, isolated from LAN		Visitor Wi-Fi VLAN
	- Implementation:
		- Firewalls enforce access between zones
		- ACLs and routing rules restrict communication
		- IDS/IPS monitor boundary traffic
	? "restrict internal network from the internet but allow public web access" -> trusted/untrusted zone separation

$ Screened Subnet (DMZ)
	- Definition: A DMZ (Demilitarized Zone) or screened subnet is a bugger network between the internal trusted network and the untrusted internet
	- It allows public-facing services (like web, email, or DNS) to be accessed without exposing the internal LAN
	- Architecture Options
		- Single Firewall (Three-legged)
			- internet -> firewall -> untrusted, DMZ, trusted
		- Dual Firewall
			- internet --- edge firewall --- DMZ --- Internal Firewall --- LAN
		- Edge firewall -> Filters external traffic
		- Internal firewall -> Protects LAN from DMZ breaches
	- Security Principle:
		- Limit inbound/outbound rules between zones
		- Internal LAN never directly accessible from Internet
		- Public servers (web, mail, DNS) live in DMZ
	- Common services in a DMZ:
		- Web servers
		- Email gateways
		- Reverse Proxies
		- VPN concentrators
	- Advantages:
		- Reduces blast radius of compromise
		- Supports least privilege between networks
		- Simplifies monitoring and logging
	? "public web server accessible to the internet but isolated from internal systems" -> screened subnet / DMZ


