<Troubleshooting Methodology>
*** NOTES ***
$ Step 1 (Identify the Problem)
- Goal: Gather as much information as possible to understand the scope and symptoms of the issue
- What to Do:
	- Question the user: What changed, what they observed, when it started
	- Identify symptoms: e.g., no connectivity, slow performance, intermittent drops
	- Determine if anything changed recently: New updates, hardware, network policies
	- Establish the scope: Is it one user, one subnet, or the entire network?
	- Duplicate the problem: Can you reproduce it?
	- Check device status indicators: Link lights, LEDs, error messages
	- Review logs and alerts: Switch, router, firewall, or server logs
- Tools and Data
	- Ping, ipconfig/ifconfig, traceroute
	- Network monitoring logs (Syslog, SNMP)
	- User reports and tickets

$ Step 2 (Establish a Theory of Probably Cause)
- Goal: Come up with possible reasons for the issue - start broad, then narrow down
- What to Consider:
	- Is it hardware-related? (bad cable, failed port)
	- Configuration-related?: (VLAN mismatch, wrong IP/gateway)
	- Software-related?: (Update issue, driver failure)
	- External causes?: (ISP outage, DoS attack, power issue)
? "You suspect a faulty switch port" -> Theory stage (haven't confirmed it yet)

$ Step 3: Test the Theory to Determine the Cause
- Goal: Validate or disprove your theory with tests or diagnostics
- What to Do:
	- Run ping/traceroute to confirm connectivity
	- Check interface status on switches and routers
	- Swap cables or ports (simple isolation test)
	- Try different devices on the same connection
	- Review logs to match error timestamps
- If the theory is confirmed -> move to step 4
- If not -> got back to Step 2

$ Step 4: Establish a Plan of Action and Identify Potential Effects
- Goal: Plan how to fix the issue - safely, without disrupting other systems
- What to Include:
	- The fix steps
	- Rollback Plan 
	- Approval if needed
	- Identify downtime impacts or user disruption
? If your "Deciding when to apply a fix" or "getting approval to make a change" that's planning, not implementing

$ Step 5: Implement the Solution or Escalate
- Goal: Execute the fix - or, if beyond your authority/expertise, escalate to the appropriate team
- What to Do:
	- Appd
\ly the configuration change, replacement, or patch
	- Follow organizational change management procedures
	- Document exactly what was done
- If escalation is required:
	- Provide all gathered data (symptoms, steps tried, results)j
? "After confirming the cause, the tech installs a new switch module" -> Implementation

$ Step 6 (Verify Full System Functionality and Implement Preventive Measures)
- Goal: Ensure the issue is fully resolved and doesn't reoccur
-j What to Do:
	- Test all affected systems and services
	- Verify user functionality (can they connect and perform tasks?)
	- Monitor logs and network performance
	- Implement preventative measures:
		- Update firmware/software
		- Add redundancy
		- Adjust configurations
? You confirm that users can now connect to the internet after replacing the router -> Verify functionality

$ Step 7: Document Findings, Actions, and Outcomes:
- Goal: Record what happened, what was done, wand what fixed it - for future reference and auditing
- What to Document:
	- Problem description
	- Root cause
	- Steps taken to resolve
	- Preventative measures implemented
	- Verification results
	- Time/date and who performed the fix
- Purpose:
	- Builds organizational knowledge base
	- Speeds up future troubleshooting
	- Supports change and incident management policies
? "The issue is resolved what should you do next?" -> Document Findings

<Cabling and Physical Interface Issues>
*** NOTES ***
$ Cable Issues
- Goal: Identify and correct Layer 1 (Physical) problems that prevent signal transmission or cause poor performance
	- a. Incorrect Cable Type	
		- Problem: Using the wrong category or type of cable for a given speed, distance, or purpose
			- Example                                   | Why it's Wrong           | Symptom
			Cat5 used for Gigabit Ethernet              | Not rated for 1000 Mbps  | Low throughput, link instability
			Straight-through used instead of crossover  | TX/RX not aligned        | No connectivity
			Multimode Fiber used instead of single-mode | Signal loss, attenuation | Link down, no light
			Patch (stranded) cable used for long runs   | higher resistance        | Intermittent connection
		? "Network link between two switches fails to establish; cable labeled Cat5 is being used for a Gigabit uplink" -> Replace with Cat5e or higher
	- b. Signal Degradation (Attenuation, EMI, and Crosstalk)
		- Signal degradation occurs when electrical or optical signals weaken or distort over distance
		- Causes: 
			- Long cable runs exceeding spec limits
				- Ethernet limit: 100 meters for copper (90m horizontal + 10m patch)
			- Electromagnetic Interference (EMI)
				- Caused by motors, fluorescent lights, microwaves, power cables
			- Crosstalk:
				- Interference between twisted pairs
		- Symptoms:
			- Intermittent Connectivity
			- Frame erros, CRC Errors
			- Slow transfer speeds
		- Tools to Detect
			- Cable certifier or TDR (Time Domain Reflectometer)
			- Network Analyzer (measures signal quality)
		- Fix:
			- Keep data cables away from power lines
			- Use shielded twisted pair (STP) or fiber in noisy environments
			- Respect max cable lengths
	 - c. Improper Termination
		- Problem: RJ45 connectors not terminated correctly (bad pinout or poor crimping)
		T568A - Green pair first - used in residential
		T568B - Orange pair first - used in most commercial devices
		- Symptoms:
			- No link light
			- Collisions, CRC errors
			- Link flapping (connects, disconnects)
		- Fix:
			- Re-crimp or re-terminate ends using proper color order and high-quality connectors
		? "Network drops occur after a user replaced an RJ45 connector. What's the likely cause" -> Improper Termination
	- d. TX/RX Transposed
		- Tx (Transmit) and RX (Receive) lines are reversed - meaning the devices can't 'hear' each other
		- Fiber: TX on one side must go to RX on the other
		- Copper: Older switches/routers without auto-MDI-X need crossover
		- Symptoms:
			- No link lights
			- Interface down/down
			- No data transfer even if link light on
		- Fix:
			- Reverse the pair (swap TX/RX)
			- Use crossover cable or enable auto-MDI/MDI-X
		? "Fiber link light on one side but not the other" -> TX/RX Reversed

$ Interface Issues
- These happen on the network device's port (switch, router, NIC)
- Monitoring interface counters helps detect subtle Layer 1-2 issues
	- a. Increasing Counters
		- Interfaces maintain counters for frames, errors, and drops.
		- When these values increase abnormally -> Indicates a problem
		Counter             | Indicates                | Possible Cause
		CRC errors          | Bad frames               | EMI, bad cable
		Collisions          | Shared medium issue      | Half-duplex mismatch
		Late Collisions     | Out-of-spec cabling      | Cable too long
		Runts               | Frames too short         | Duplex mismatch
		Giants              | Frames too large         | Jumbo frame mismatch
		Input/Output errors | Physical interface issue | Bad port or transceiver
	- Fix:
		- Replace cable or transceiver
		- Check duplex/speed negotiation
		- Verify interface configuration
	? "An admin notices rapidly increasing CRC errors" -> Check cable quality or EMI first
	
	- b. Port Status Indicators
		- Modern network interfaces have LED indicators that show real-time status
		LED Pattern    | Meaning
		Solid Green    | Link up and active
		Flashing Green | Link active, traffic passing
		Amber Orange   | Low speed (10/100 Mbps), errors, PoE Fault
		Off            | No connection or power
	- Fix:
		- Check cable connection and port enablement
		- Verify NIC enabled and correct VLAN assigned
	? "The link light is off on one switch port" -> Problem likely Layer 1 (cable or port)

$ Hardware Issues
- Sometimes the physical device itself is to blame - not the cable or configuration
	- a. PoE (Power over Ethernet) Problems
		- Powers devices like APs, IP phones, and cameras over the Ethernet Cable
		Issue                  | Symptom                | Fix
		Power budget exceeded  | New device won't power | Disconnect or upgrade switch
		Incorrect PoE standard | Device not powering    | Match IEEE spec (802.3af/at/bt)
		Bad Cable              | Voltage Drop           | Replace with quality Cat5e/6 cable
	- Standards Recap
		Standard        | Power (at PD) | Example Device
		802.3af (poE)   | 15.4W         | IP Phone
		802.3at (PoE+)  | 25.5W         | Wireless AP
		802.3bt (PoE++) | 60-100W       | PTZ Cameras
	- b. Transceiver Mismatch
		- Transceivers (SFP, SFP+< QSFP, etc.) must match both the device and the cable/fiber type
		Mismatch Type                                | Symptom
		SFP (1G) in SFP+ (10G) Port                  | Port won't link
		Single-mode transceiver with multimode fiber | Signal loss
		Vendor Mismatch                              | "Unsupported Transceiver" errors
		- Fix:
			- Use manufacturer-approved transceivers
			- Match speed, wavelength, and mode
	- c. Signal strength (for Wireless or Fiber)
		- Weak signal = unstable or slow connection
		- Causes:
			- Damaged fiber
			- Poor alignment
			- Wireless AP too far / obstructions
		- Tools:
			- Optical power Meter (fiber)
			- Wi-Fi analyzer (wireless)
		- Fixes:
			- Clean fiber connectors
			- Reroute fiber with gentle bends
			- Reposition antennas / APs for better coverage
		? "Users report intermittent Wi-Fi drops; signal strength shows -80 dBm" -> Weak wireless signal


<Network Services Issues>
*** NOTES ***
$ Switching Issues (Layer 2)
	- Switching problems can prevent communication within the same network.
	- They often manifest as connectivity failures, VLAN isolation, or loop-induced outages
	- a. Spanning Tree Protocol Issues
		- Purpose: STP prevents layer 2 loops by blocking redundant switch paths until they're needed
		- Key Concepts
			- STP identifies a root bridge and blocks redundant links
			- Port states: Blocking -> Listening -> Learning -> Forwarding
			- BDPU (Bridge Protocol Data Units): Used to elect root bridge and detect loops
		- Common Issues
	- b. VLAN Assignment Issues
		- VLANs isolate broadcast domains
		- If a port is in the wrong VLAN -> It's like it's on a different network
		- Symptoms:
			- Device's cant ping others on same switch
			- Trunk ports show mismatched VLANs
			- DHCP requests fail (broadcasts blocked by VLAN boundaries)
		- How to Check:
			- On Cisco: show vlan brief or show interface switch-port
			- Ensure access ports are in the correct VLAN
			- Trunk ports carry correct VLANs (check allowed VLAN list)
		- Fix:
			- Correct VLAN membership
			- Ensure trunks use same native VLAN on both ends
			- Verify VLAN exists and is active on all switches
		? "User can't access local server; both are cabled to the same switch" -> Port VLAN mismatch
	- c. ACL (Access Control List) Misconfiguration
		- ACLs are used to permit or deny traffic based on IPs, protocols, or ports
		- Common Issues:
			- Users can't reach server -> ACL blocking required ports
			- Ping fails between VLANs -> ACL denying ICMP or inter-VLAN traffic
			- DHCP not working -> ACL blocking UDP 67/68 broadcast
		- How to Check:
			- show access-lists or show up interface
			- Confirm ACL direction (inbound/outbound)
			- Remember: ACLs process top-down - first match wins
		- Fix:
			- Adjust rules or reorder ACL entries
			- Ensure an explicit "permit" rule exists for intended traffic
			- Verify placement on correct interface and direction
		? "Ping fails between subnets after security update" -> ACL blocking inter-VLAN or routed traffic

$ Routing Issues (Layer 3)
	- Routing Determines how packets move between networks.
	- If something's off in the routing table, packets die en route
	- a. Routing Table Issues
		- The Routing Table:
			- Stores known networks and next-hop IPs
			- Includes directly connected, static, and dynamically learned routes
		- Common Problems:
			- Missing route: Destination unreachable
			- Wrong next-hop: Packets routed incorrectly
			- Asymmetric Routing: Replies never return
			- Overlapping Subnets: Routing confusion, traffic misdelivery
		- How to check:
			- show ip route (Cisco) or ip route (Linux)
			- Verify routes exist for all needed destinations
			- Ping next-hop gateway for reachability
		- Fix:
			- Add or correct static routes
			- Ensure routing protocols (OSPF/BGP/EIGRP) are exchanging updates properly
			- Remove duplicate or conflicting routes
		? "Traceroute stops after router A; router B has no route to 10.0.0.0/16" -> Missing entry route
	- b. Default Route Issues
		- The default route 0.0.0.0/0 handles all unknown destinations
		- Common Problems:
			- Default route missing -> internet unreachable
			- Wrong next-hop -> Packets black-holed
			- Overridden by more specific route
		- Fix:
			- Add correct default route
			- Verify NAT/firewall not blocking return traffic
		? Internal clients can't access the internet, but local routing works -> Missing or wrong default route

$ Address Pool Exhaustion
	- Address Pools are used by DHCP servers, NAT devices, and cloud VPCs
	- When they run out of available addresses, new clients fail to connect
	- a. DHCP Pool Exhaustion
		- Symptoms:
			- Clients get APIPA addresses (169.254.x.x)
			- New users can't join the network
			- DHCP logs show "no available addresses"
		- Causes:
			- Too small DHCP scope
			- Lease times too long
			- Rouge DHCP server
			- Too many devices on subnet
		- Fix:
			- Expand DHCP scope
			- Shorted lease durations
			- Identify and disable rogue DHCP servers
			- Subnet the network to add more address pools
		? "Clients are getting 169.254.x.x addresses" -> DHCP pool exhausted or DHCP unavailable
	- b. NAT Pool Exhaustion
		- For devices using dynamic NAT, the router maintains a pool of public IPs
		- If all are in use -> new outbound sessions fail
		- Symptoms:
			- Some users can browse internet, others cant
			- Logs show NAT sessions and clear them
		? "Some users lose Internet access at peak hours" -> NAT pool exhausted

$ Incorrect Gateway / IP / Subnet Mask
	- These are Layer 3 addressing issues that cause local or remote communication failures.
	- a. Incorrect IP Address
		- Symptom:
			- Ip conflict or wrong network
			- "Duplicate IP detected" errors
			- Can't reach local or remote resources
		- Fix: 
			- Reassign IP manually or via DHCP
			- Use IPAM to track and prevent overlaps
	- b. Wrong Subnet Mask
		- Symptom:
			- Can ping some hosts but not others on same network
			- Broadcasts not received by all devices
		- Fix:
			- Verify mask matches subnet design
			- Update static configs or DHCP scopes
		? "A device can only reach half of its local subnet" -> Incorrect Subnet Mask
	- c. Incorrect Default Gateway
		- Symptom:
			- Local communication works, but Internet Unreachable
			- Traceroute stops at first hop
		- Fix:
			- Set gateway to router interface on same subnet
			- Verify ARP entry resolves to correct MAC
		? "User can access local file server but not external sites" -> Wrong default gateway
	- Summary
		Issue Type       | Common Symptom               | Root Cause                  | Fix
		STP              | Loops, outages               | STP disabled/misplaced root | Re-enable STP, adjust priorities
		VLAN             | Can't talk to same switch    | Wrong VLAN membership       | Correct port VLANs
		ACL              | Blocked Traffic              | Rule Order / Direction      | Adjust ACLs
		Routing Table    | "Destination Unreachable"    | Missing route               | Add static/dynamic route
		Default Route    | No Internet                  | Wrong next-hop              | Correct default gateway
		DHCP Pool        | APIPA 169.254.x.x            | Exhausted leases            | Expand scope
		NAT Pool         | Some users fail              | Out of public IPs           | Use PAT or enlarge pool
		Wrong IP/Mask/GW | Partial or no connectivity   | Manual misconfig            | Correct addressing
	- Real World troubleshooting flow
		- 1. Ping local gateway -> tests local VLAN + IP config
		- 2. Ping remote IP -> Tests routing and gateway
		- 3. Ping external (8.8.8.8) -> tests Nat and ISP
		- 4. Ping domain (google.com) -> tests DNS
	
<Performance Issues>
*** NOTES ***
$ Network Congestion
	- What it is:
		- Congestion occurs when too much traffic sent through a network path or device, exceeding available bandwidth or buffer capacity
		- When a switch, router, or link can't process packets fast enough -> packets are queued or dropped
	- Symptoms:
		- Slow network speeds, especially at peak times.
		- High latency and jitter (voice/video distortion)
		- Packet drops and retransmissions (seen with TCP)
		- CPU Spikes on routers or firewalls
	- How to Identify:
		- SNMP monitoring: High interface utilization
		- NetFlow/sFlow: Which IPs or apps use most bandwidth
		- show interfaces: High output drops, queue depth
		- Wireshark: TCP retransmissions, window size reduction
	- Causes:
		- Insufficient link capacity (100 Mbps uplink with 200 Mbps demand)
		- Broadcast storms
		- Misconfigured QoS (voice/video not prioritized)
		- Malware or DoS attacks flooding traffic
	- Fixes:
		- 1. Identify top talkers -> use NetFlow or packet capture
`		- 2. Increase bandwidth (upgrade link or add parallel paths)
		- 3. Implement QoS -> prioritize time-sensitive traffic (VoIP, video)
		- 4. Segment traffic -> VLANs or subnets for heavy users
		- 5. Check Loops -> ensure STP prevents broadcast storms
	? "Users complain of slow network performance around 9 a.m daily" -> Look for bandwidth congestion

$ Latency
	- What it is:
		- Latency: Time taken for a packet to travel from source to destination and back (RTT - round-trip time) (measured in ms)
		- A healthy LAN has <5ms latency. WANs or internet paths may be 20-100+ ms
	- Symptoms:
		- Voice calls sound delayed or robotic
		- Video streaming buffers
		- File transfers take longer than expected
		- Ping shows high response times
	- How to Measure:
		Tool            | Example Command    | Output
		Ping            | ping 8.8.8.8       | RTT in ms
		Traceroute      | tracert 8.8.8.8    | Which hop adds delay
		MTR (Linux)     | mtr google.com     | Combined ping + trace view
		Network monitor | SNMP latency graphs
	- Causes:
		- Network congestion (queueing delay)
		- Long physical distance (propagation delay)
		- Misconfigured QoS or routing loops
		- VPN encryption overhead
		- Faulty cables or bad optics
	- Fixes:
		- Check traceroute -> locate where latency spikes (router hop)
		- Verify routing path -> ensure shortest, most efficient route
		- Enable QoS for delay-sensitive apps
		- Upgrade WAN bandwidth or optimize routing (SD-WAN)
		- Eliminate unnecessary VPN hops
	? "Users in a branch office experience 250ms latency to headquarters" -> Wan link distance or congestion. Not a DNS or IP conflict

$ Packet Loss
 	- What is It:
		- When packets are dropped (never arrive at destination)
		- TCP retransmits them, but UDP (voice/video) does not - causing stuttering or gaps
		- Even small loss rates (1%-2%) can cripple performance for real-time applications
	- Symptoms:
		- Voice/video jitter or drops
		- Downloads fail or timeout
		- Applications randomly disconnect
		- Ping shows intermittent "Request timed out"
	- How to Detect:
		- Ping: Observe packet loss percentage
		- Traceroute: See which hop loses packets
		- Wireshark: "TCP Retransmission" flags
	`	- SNMP/Interface stats: CRC errors, output drops
		- Speed test tools: Measure loss under load
	- Causes:
		- Faulty cable, connector, or port (CRC errors)
		- Wireless interference or low signal
		- Network congestion (queues overflow)
		- Duplex mismatch (half vs full)
		- MTU mismatch (oversized packets dropped)
		- Firewall or ACL dropping packets
	- Fixes:
		- Check interface stats (show interfaces) -> CRC or drop counts
		- Replace cable or switch port
		- Check duplex/speed settings -> force full-duplex if mismatched
		- Enable jumbo frames consistently if using them
		- Monitor QoS queues for congestion drops.
	? "Ping test shows 10% packet loss; interface has CRC errors" -> layer 1 issue - replace cable or port

$ Wireless Interference
	- What it Is:
		- Interference occurs when multiple wireless signals overlap or environmental factors distort signals.
		- It results in packet loss, retransmissions, and high latency
	- Frequency bands
		- 2.4 GHz: Microwaves, cordless phones, Bluetooth, other wi-fi
		- 5 GHz: Fewer sources, but shorter range
		- 6 GHz (Wi-fi 6e): Clean spectrum, but limited device support.
	- Symptoms:
		- Slow wi-fi speeds even with strong signal
		- Clients disconnect intermittently
		- High retry count or ping spikes
		- Users near microwaves or Bluetooth devices affected
	- Tools to Identify:
		- Wi-Fi analyzer (NetSpot, Ekahau, Wireshark): Detect overlapping channels
		- Spectrum Analyzer: Identify non-wi-fi interference
		- Controller Dashboard: View channel utilization and noise floor
	- Causes:
		- Overlapping channels (especially on 2.4 GHz)
		- Co-channel interference (too many APs on same channel)
		- Non-wifi-devices emitting on same band
		- Physical obstructions (walls, metal, glass)
		- APs placed too close (signal overlap)
	- Fixes:
		- Reassign channels:
			- 2.4 GHz -> use only 1, 6, 11 (non-overlapping)
			- 5 GHz -> Use DFS channels if available
		- Reduce AP transmit power to avoid overlap
		- Reposition APs for even coverage
		- Upgrade clients/APs to 5GHz or 6GHz
		- Perform site survey to optimize placement
	? "Users report wi-fi slowdowns when multiple APs are active" -> Channel overlap or co-channel interference.
	- Summary:
		Issue                 | OSI Layer | Main Symptom      | Common Cause                | Key Fix
		Congestion            | 3/4       | Network slowdown  | Too much traffic            | QoS, add bandwidth
		Latency               | 3         | High ping/jitter  | distance, congestion        | optimize path, qos
		Packet Loss           | 1-4       | Drops/Retransmits | Bad cable, interference     | Replace hardware, fix duplex
		Wireless Interference | 1         | Unstable wi-fi    | Overlapping channels, noise | Reassign channels, site survey


<Tools and Protocols>
*** NOTES ***
$ Protocol Analyzers
	- What they are:
		- A protocol analyzer (also called a packet sniffer) captures, decodes, and analyzes network packets traveling over a network interface
		- These Tools let you see exactly what's happening on the wire - the actual frames, headers, and payloads between devices
	- Common Tools:
		- Wireshark: Most popular open-source packet analyzer. GUI based
		- tcpdump: CLI version (Linux/macOS). Capture and filter packets via command line
		- Tshark: Wiresharks cli equivalent
		- SolarWinds Network Analyzer: Commercial option with dashboards
	- What you can see:
		- TCP handshakes (SYN, SYN-ACK, ACK)
		- DNS queries and responses
		- ARP requests and replies
		- ICMP pings and errors
		- HTTP/HTTPS requests
		- DHCP leases
		- CNMP traffic
	- Common Uses:
		Scenario             | What to Look For
		Slow connections     | TCP retransmissions or excessive ACK delays
		DNS issues           | Query packets and response IPs
		DHCP issues          | Discover / Offer / Request / Ack sequence
		Suspicious traffic   | unusual ports or IPs (malware, scanning)
		VoIP troubleshooting | SIP packets and jitter patterns
	? "Which tool would you use to view DNS request packets in real time?" -> Protocol Analyzer (Wireshark or tcpdump)

$ Command-Line Tools
	- a. ping
		- Purpose: Tests basic connectivity and latency between devices using ICMP Echo Request/Reply
		- Key insights:
			- Success = host reachable
			- Failure = network, routing, or firewall issue
			- High RTT (ms) = latency or congestion
		? if ping to IP works but not to hostname -> DNS problem
	- b. traceroute / tracert
		- Purpose: shows the path packets take to a destination (each hop)
		- Windows: tracert 8.8.8.8
		- Linux/macOS: traceroute 8.8.8.8
		- Use cases
			- Identify where latency spikes occur
			- Detect routing loops or black holes
			- Verify ISP or WAN path
		? If traceroute stops at first hop -> default gateway issues
		? If stops mid-path -> ISP or firewall filtering ICMP
	- c. ipconfig / ifconfig / ip
		- Windows: ipconfig: View IP, mask, gateway, DNS
		- Linux / macOS: ifconfig: or set IP  (deprecated)
		- Modern Linux: ip addr, ip route: Modern replacement for ifconfig
		- Key Flags (Windows)
			- ipconfig /all -> Detailed info
			- ipconfig /release -> Drop DHCP lease
			- ipconfig /renew -> Get new DHCP IP
			- ipconfig /flushdns -> Clear DNS cache
		? If device shows 169.254.x.x IP -> DHCP issue (no lease)
	- d. nslookup/dig
		- Purpose: Test and verify DNS resolution
		- nslookup google.com
		- dig google.com
		- Use Cases:
			- Confirm DNS server functionality
			- Verify domain-to-IP mappings
			- Check for internal vs external DNS leaks
		? if nslookup fails but ping by IP works -> DNS problem
	- e. netstat
		- Purpose: Display active connections, listening ports, and routing tables
		- netstat -an 
		- netstat -rn -> show routing table
		- netstat -s -> show statistics
		- Use Cases:
			- Identify open ports (security check)
			- Find established TCP sessions
			- Troubleshoot default routes
	- f. arp
		- Purpose: view or manipulate the ARP cache (maps IPs to MAC addresses)
		- arp -a -> show current arp table
		- arp -d * -> clear arp cache
		? If device can't reach local subnet -> bad ARP entry or MAC table issue
	- g. nmap
		- Purpose: Scan a host or subnet for open ports, services, and os fingerprints
			- nmap 192.168.1.0/24	
			- nmap -p 22, 80 10.0.0.5
		- Use Cases:
			- Discover devices on a network
			- Check firewall rules
			- Detect unauthorized hosts
		? "Which tool determines what ports are open on a remote host?" -> nmap
	- h tcpdump
		- Purpose: CLI packet capture (like Wireshark without GUI)
		- tcpdump -i eth0 port 80
		- tcpdump -n host 8.8.8.8
		? used to capture and analyze live traffic - perfect for headless systems or quick troubleshooting
	- i. show commands (Cisco)net
		- show interfaces -> check link, errors, CRC
		- show ip route -> Routing table
		- show vlan brief -> VLAN assignment
		- show spanning-tree -> STP state
		- show mac address-table -> Layer 2 mapping
		? if CRC errors increasing -> back cable or EMI (Layer 1)

$ Cable Testers
	- What They Are:
		- Physical layer tools to test copper and fiber connections for continuity, pinout, and signal quality
	- a. Continuity Tester
		- Simple pass/fail check: ensures each conductor is connected end-to-end
		- Detects opens, shorts, crossed pairs
	- b. Wire Map Tester
		- Verifies correct pinout (T568A/B standard)
		- Detects TX/RX reversed, split pairs, or miswiring
		? Which tool verifies that each wire in a Cat6 cable is properly terminated?" -> Wire map tester
	- c. Time Domain Reflectometer (TDR)
		- Measures cable length and locates breaks or shorts using reflected signals
		- Reports fault distance in meters/feet
		- For Fiber: user Optical TDR (OTDR)
		? "Which tool locates a cable break 57m away?" -> TDR
	-d. Cable Certifier
		- High-end tool used by installers to certify cable meets performance standards (Cat6, Cat6a)
		- Measures attenuation, NEXT, and signal loss
		? Used in professional installations to guarantee performance
	- e. Tone Generator and Probe ("Fox and Hound")
		- Sends a tone down a cable -> probe detects which cable emits the tone
		- Used for cable tracing in patch panels or walls
		? "Technician needs to located one cable among many" -> Tone generator and probe

$ Wi-Fi Analyzers
	- What They Are:
		- Tools that scan the wireless spectrum to detect signal strength, SSIDs, channels, interference, and coverage overlap
	- Examples:
		Tool                          | Type
		Ekahau / NetSpot / AirMagnet  | Professional Wi-Fi site survey tools
		Wireshark (with monitor mode) | Packet Capture for Wi-Fi
		Acrylic Wi-Fi analyzer        | Free windows signal visualizer
		Mobile Apps                   | Android/iOS Wi-Fi analyzers for signal mapping
	- What You Can Find:
		Issue                   | Detected by
		Weak signal strength    | RSSI values (below -70 dBm = poor)
		Channel overlap         | Overlapping SSIDs on 1/6/11
		Co-channel interference | Too many APs on same channel
		Rogue APs               | Unauthorized SSIDs
		Noise sources           | Spectrum analyzer readings
	- Fixes and uses:
		- Reassign channels (1, 6, 11 for 2.4Ghz)
		- Reposition APs for better coverage
		- Adjust transmit power to avoid overlap
		- Identify and disable rogue access points
		? "Which tool identifies overlapping Wi-Fi channels in an office?" -> Wi-Fi analyzer
	
